{"raw": {"https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor---Emerging-Leader-in-Arctic-governance--Indigenous-leadership--community-resilience--or-related-fields_JR38816-1": {"title": "Assistant Professor - Emerging Leader in Arctic governance, Indigenous leadership, community resilience, or related fields", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nFull time\n\nJob Type: Academic Regular Faculty/Department: Faculty of Social Sciences_FT The University of Ottawa (uOttawa)\u202f invites applications for a tenure-track Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader position in Arctic governance, Indigenous leadership, community resilience, or related fields , aligned with the Government of Canada strategic priority area of environment, climate resilience and the Arctic . This position is associated with the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair of Canada in Arctic Governance and Community Resilience in the Faculty of Social Sciences , as part of the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chairs of Canada Program ( formerly Canada Impact+ Research Chairs Program). \u00a0 \u00a0 The Arctic is undergoing profound environmental, geopolitical, and societal change, creating an urgent need for innovative governance approaches. The University of Ottawa (uOttawa) invites applications for a tenure-track Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader position from scholars working in Arctic governance, Indigenous leadership, community resilience, or related fields. The position is associated with the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair of Canada in Arctic Governance and Community Resilience under the Canada Impact+ Research Chairs Program and aligns with the Government of Canada's strategic priority area of environment, climate resilience, and the Arctic.\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 The Faculties of Social Sciences and Arts seek an emerging scholar whose expertise strengthens and expands the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair ' s research program. The appointee will contribute to interdisciplinary scholarship that integrates diverse knowledge systems, governance approaches, and collaborative research methods. Working in partnership with Indigenous and northern organizations and communities as well as a network of international partners, they will co-develop innovative governance solutions to complex Arctic challenges. This work will advance Indigenous leadership, strengthen community resilience, and inform decision-making across local, regional, national, and international scales. \u00a0 \u00a0 The successful candidate will join the uOttawa Arctic Hub, an interdisciplinary initiative connecting researchers from across the university with Indigenous organizations, governments, northern communities, and international partners. The Hub provides an exceptional environment for partnership-based research, policy engagement, and knowledge mobilization in support of evidence-informed decision-making and meaningful societal impact. \u00a0 \u00a0 Preference will be given to candidates with demonstrated experience translating scholarship into policy and practice through partnerships with Indigenous organizations, governments, communities, and other societal partners. The ideal candidate will demonstrate a commitment to collaborative, interdisciplinary, and partnership-based research with tangible societal impact, and the ability to bridge diverse knowledge systems in addressing complex Arctic challenges. \u00a0 uOttawa is ranked fifth in research intensity in 2025 and is renowned for\u202fpioneering discoveries across multiple disciplines. Several of our research centers and institutes rank among the top 100 globally, including the top six in Canada.\u202f \u00a0 Located in Canada\u2019s vibrant capital, uOttawa , proud to be the world\u2019s largest bilingual (English and French) university, is minutes away from federal and other government institutions, several national agencies, embassies, international organizations, and connected to the thriving high-tech sector. We are committed to building partnerships across Canada and internationally and fostering innovation and knowledge mobilization in areas of significant social and economic impact. \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 About the Canada Impact + Emerging Leaders Program \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 The Eddie Goldenberg Research Chairs of Canada Program is a very prestigious, one-time initiative designed to support universities in attracting world-leading researchers whose work addresses critical national and global challenges. The program also includes Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders awards , a $120M investment to help institutions recruit internationally based early career researchers to Canada. \u00a0 \u00a0 The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders award provides $100,000 per year over six years, with the possibility of a six-year funded extension at the same yearly amount, subject to program conditions. \u00a0 T he Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader must be recruited within the same strategic priority area as the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chairholder, but the early career researcher (ECR) and the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chairholder don't necessarily need to be in the same discipline or research field or be aligned with the same federal research funding agency. \u00a0 The ECR nominees are expected to have an independent program of research, with potential for impact through collaborations with, but not limited to, the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chairholders. Institutions may include support for the ECR in their research infrastructure request to the Canada Foundation for Innovation (CFI) through the Impact+ Research Infrastructure fund associated with the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair . \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 * Note: An early career researcher is a researcher within five years from the date of their first research-related appointment, minus eligible delays in research, where: \u00a0 research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently; and \u00a0 all eligible leaves (e.g., maternity, parental, medical, bereavement) are credited as twice the amount of time taken, and professional leaves (e.g., training, sabbatical, administrative) are not credited. \u00a0 \u00a0 Eligibility of Candidates: \u00a0 \u00a0 Only candidates who are internationally based (both working and residing outside of Canada) when the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair is accepted are eligible. Candidates who are at uOttawa or are based in Canada are not eligible to apply for this position as stipulated by the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chairs of Canada Program . Candidates must also meet the program\u2019s early career researcher eligibility requirements . \u00a0 \u00a0 The successful candidate will be expected to begin their full-time tenure-track appointment at uOttawa by July 3, 2027. \u00a0 \u00a0 Skills requirements: \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Education: Ph.D (please note that this is a requirement from the institution, and not the program) . \u00a0 Required Qualifications : superior research achievements in the Impact+ Emerging Leader research area and the relevant Government of Canada strategic priority area; evidence of interdisciplinary collaborations, a solid record with respect to teaching and training of students at the graduate level, the ability to obtain external research funds . Excellent communication skills in either English or French. A ctive knowledge of one\u2019s second O fficial L anguage (French or English) is a requirement for tenure. English-French bilingualism at the time of appointment is an asset. \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Application Package: \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 A cover letter (1-2 pages) highlighting the candidate\u2019s research theme and how it fits with the Eddie Goldenberg Research Chairs of Canada Program and the Government of Canada\u2019s strategic priority areas , how it aligns with uOttawa\u2019s Strategic Areas of Research and research strengths , and the candidate\u2019s qualifications and interest in being considered for a Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader position at u Ottawa. \u00a0 An up-to-date curriculum vitae (Please include any personal circumstances and/or career interruptions such as parental or sick leaves that may have impacted your research trajectory. These will be taken into consideration in the selection process); \u00a0 \u00a0 A research plan (2 to 3 pages); \u00a0 \u00a0 Potential for knowledge translation and mobilization (1 page) to include: \u00a0 How the research results could be shared and applied in practices, products and/or services, and how tangible economic or societal benefits for Canada and Canadians will be generated. \u00a0 Outline the proposed engagement mechanisms, target users, and potential partnerships such as private and public sectors, national and international research institutions, philanthropic organizations and Indigenous communities (if applicable). \u00a0 A statement of teaching interests (1 to 2 pages); \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 A statement on equity, diversity, and inclusion philosophy and concrete practices (1 page); \u00a0 \u00a0 The names and contact information of three people who may be contacted by the University for letters of reference (i.e. one arm\u2019s length and two who are not affiliated to u Ottawa unless they are the candidate\u2019s PhD or postdoctoral supervisor ) ; \u00a0 \u00a0 Self-identification: Complete the confidential Employment Equity questionnaire online during the application process. Further information about the collection of self-identification information can be found in the guidelines on \u201c Creating an Equitable, Diverse and Inclusive Research Environment: A Best Practices Guide for Recruitment, Hiring and Retention \u201d. \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Commitment to Equity, Diversity and Inclusion: \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Located on the ancestral land of the Algonquin people, u Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from individuals from all underrepresented, equity-seeking, rights-seeking populations, including Indigenous peoples, racialized individuals, persons with disabilities, women and individuals from LGBTQ2+ communities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively implement equity measures that ensure and retain diverse teams and promote inclusive practices for research team members. \u00a0 \u00a0 Duties : Academic staff functions include, in varying proportions: teaching ; scholarly activities such as research, artistic or literary creation, or professional work ; academic service ; and graduate student supervision . \u00a0 \u00a0 Rank and salary : \u00a0 New tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant P rofessor . The starting salary for an Assistant Professor is $99,377 (according to the scale in effect as of May 1, 2025). \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Benefits package : The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, the University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses. \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Location of work: Faculty of Social Sciences , University of Ottawa, Ottawa, ON K1N 6N5 . \u00a0 \u00a0 Deadline: October 4 , 2026. \u00a0 \u00a0 More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . \u00a0 Apply online through the University of Ottawa Careers portal. \u00a0 To apply, we invite you to merge the various sections of your application into a single PDF file, which you can then upload to the Careers website. However, if you encounter any problems submitting your application, you can send it to the following email address: chaires_chairs@uottawa.ca . \u00a0 \u00a0 The selection process will begin immediately after the closing date and will continue until the position is filled. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Please address your cover letter to the following person: \u00a0 \u00a0 Dr. Julie St-Pierre, Vice-President, Research and Innovation \u00a0 \u00a0 University of Ottawa \u00a0 \u00a0 550 Cumberland St., room 246 \u00a0 \u00a0 Ottawa, ON K1N 6N5 CANADA \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 For questions, please email: chaires_chairs@uottawa.ca \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Ad posted on August 28, 2026 , modified on September 4 . \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. \u00a0 If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by emailing to St\u00e9fanie Berg\u00e8s-Lalonde at the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations, by using the following email address: vra.affairesprofessorales@uOttawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its more than 175-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides second-language training to staff members and their spouses."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor---Democracy-and-Technology_JR38846": {"title": "Assistant Professor - Democracy and Technology", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Graduate School of Public and International Affairs_FT Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/02 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): GRADUATE SCHOOL OF PUBLIC AND INTERNATIONAL AFFAIRS FACULTY OF SOCIAL SCIENCES UNIVERSITY OF OTTAWA Assistant Professor in the field of Democracy and Technology The Graduate School of Public and International Affairs (GSPIA) in the Faculty of Social Sciences at the University of Ottawa invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the field of Democracy and Technology. Applications are invited from scholars whose research addresses the relationship between democratic governance and technology. Areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to: Digital governance and public policy Artificial intelligence and public decision-making Platform governance and democratic accountability Online political communication and disinformation Technology regulation and public institutions Surveillance, privacy, and digital rights Cybersecurity and democratic resilience Emerging technologies and governance innovation The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture and quality of life of Canada\u2019s capital. The Faculty of Social Sciences comprises nine departments, schools and institutes, which offer undergraduate, master\u2019s and doctoral programs in both English and French. With its 10,000 students, 260 full-time professors, and a wide array of programs and research centres, the Faculty of Social Sciences plays a key role at the heart of the University of Ottawa. Its graduate students are trained by excellent researchers and undertake cutting-edge research in the Faculty\u2019s Master\u2019s and Ph.D. programs. Qualifications \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Applicants must possess a Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in political science, public administration, public policy, international affairs, sociology, economics, law, communication, science and technology studies, information studies, philosophy of science, or a related discipline (completed by the time of appointment). \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Demonstrate excellence or the potential for excellence in teaching and research. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Given the mission of the GSPIA as a professional school of public policy and international affairs that educates future policy leaders, candidates should demonstrate a very strong understanding of governmental institutions, public administration, and policy-making processes in democratic societies. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Preference will be given to candidates who are able to teach in French at the time of appointment. Duties \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0The successful candidate will be expected to teach a first-year Master\u2019s level course in \u201c Democratic Government and Public Policy \u201d, among other graduate and undergraduate courses; \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Research activities; \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Supervision of graduate students; \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Participating in academic and administrative activities; \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Other activities as specified in the collective agreement. Terms New tenure-track position. Note that tenure is conditional on active knowledge of Canada's two official languages (English and French). Salary Salary scale for an Assistant Professor starts at $99,377 (as of May 1, 2025). Starting Date July 1, 2027 Benefits package The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses. Location of Work Graduate School of Public and International Affairs, University of Ottawa, 120 University, Ottawa, ON, K1N 6N5 Application Deadline The deadline for applications is\u00a0 October 31, 2026 ; however, applications may be considered after this date until the position is filled. A complete application must include the following elements: \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Curriculum vitae, \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0A letter describing their teaching and research experience and interests, \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0A clear statement of their level of fluency in French and English. Applicants must request that three referees send letters of recommendation directly to: Professor Costanza Musu Director, Graduate School of Public and International Affairs Faculty of Social Sciences University of Ottawa E-mail: \u00a0 api@uottawa.ca More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Apply online through the University of Ottawa Careers portal. The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor---Security-and-Defence-Studies_JR38809": {"title": "2 Tenured-track faculty positions - Security and Defence Studies", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Graduate School of Public and International Affairs_FT Campus: Social Sciences Building Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/02 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): GRADUATE SCHOOL OF PUBLIC AND INTERNATIONAL AFFAIRS FACULTY OF SOCIAL SCIENCES UNIVERSITY OF OTTAWA Tenure-track faculty positions in Security and Defence Studies The Graduate School of Public and International Affairs (GSPIA) in the Faculty of Social Sciences at the University of Ottawa invites applications for two (2) tenure-track faculty positions in the field of security and defence studies. The first position is open rank . The second is at the rank of Assistant Professor . Areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to: \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Canadian defence policy and military affairs \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0National security and intelligence policy and governance \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0International security policy and strategic studies \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Foreign interference and democratic resilience \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Defence procurement and industrial strategy \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Security implications of geoeconomics \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Area studies with a security and defence studies perspective \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Arctic security \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Emerging technologies and security (cybersecurity, artificial intelligence, space, autonomous systems) \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Civil-military relations \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Security implications of climate change \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0Critical infrastructure protection The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture and quality of life of Canada\u2019s capital. The Faculty of Social Sciences comprises nine departments, schools and institutes, which offer undergraduate, master\u2019s and doctoral programs in both English and French. With its 10,000 students, 260 full-time professors, and a wide array of programs and research centres, the Faculty of Social Sciences plays a key role at the heart of the University of Ottawa. Its graduate students are trained by excellent researchers and undertake cutting-edge research in the Faculty\u2019s Master\u2019s and Ph.D. programs. Qualifications \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Applicants must possess a Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in political science, public administration, public policy, international affairs, sociology, economics, law, history, or a related discipline (completed by the time of appointment). \u00b7 Demonstrate excellence or the potential for excellence in teaching and research. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Given the mission of the GSPIA as a professional school of public policy and international affairs that educates future policy leaders, candidates should demonstrate a very strong understanding of governmental institutions, public administration, and policy-making processes in democratic societies. Candidates with policy experience are encouraged to apply. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0For one of the two positions, preference will be given to candidates who are able to teach in French at the time of appointment. Duties \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Successful candidates will be expected to teach a master\u2019s level course in GSPIA\u2019s MA program in security and defence studies, among other graduate and undergraduate courses; \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Research activities; \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Supervision of graduate students; \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Participating in academic and administrative activities; \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Other activities as specified in the collective agreement. Terms Two new tenure-track position. Note that tenure is conditional on active knowledge of Canada's two official languages (English and French). Salary Salary scale for an Assistant Professor starts at $99,377 (as of May 1, 2025). Starting Date July 1, 2027 Benefits package The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses. Location of Work Graduate School of Public and International Affairs, University of Ottawa, 120 University, Ottawa, ON, K1N 6N5 Application Deadline The deadline for applications is\u00a0 October 31, 2026 ; however, applications may be considered after this date until the position is filled. A complete application must include the following elements: \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Curriculum vitae, \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0A letter describing their teaching and research experience and interests, \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0A clear statement of their level of fluency in French and English. Applicants must request that three referees send letters of recommendation directly to: Professor Costanza Musu Director, Graduate School of Public and International Affairs Faculty of Social Sciences University of Ottawa E-mail: \u00a0 api@uottawa.ca More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Apply online through the University of Ottawa Careers portal. The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Lees-Campus/Tenure-track-position-in-Neuromotor-Control-and-Motor-Learning_JR38826": {"title": "Tenure-track position in Neuromotor Control and Motor Learning", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Faculty of Health Sciences_Full Time Professors Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/02 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): As part of a major expansion of its research programs, the School of Human Kinetics (SHK) in the Faculty of Health Sciences at the University of Ottawa, invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position with scholarly expertise in the area of neuromotor control and motor learning, starting July 1st, 2027 . The SHK provides a bilingual, interdisciplinary academic and research environment in which to study. At the undergraduate level the SHK offers a Honours Bachelor of Science in Human Kinetics (BSc), emphasizing the biophysical sciences of human kinetics, with a focus on the role that biological, anatomical, musculoskeletal, and neuromotor systems have on motor performance. At the graduate level, students can complete a Master of Science or PhD in Human Kinetics, specializing in neuromotor control and motor learning. We seek a highly qualified scholar whose work advances our understanding of human movement and neuromotor control in healthy individuals and/or patient populations using experimental, computational, and/or applied techniques. The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture and quality of life of Canada\u2019s capital. Title of the positions: Assistant professor (Associate or Full Professor under certain conditions) Duties: The functions of a member of the academic staff include, in varying proportions: a) Contribute to teaching various Neuromotor Control / Motor Learning courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels in both official languages (French and English); b) Lead and sustain a competitive, externally funded program in neuromotor control and motor learning-related research involving graduate student supervision; c) Contribute to regular administrative processes at the School, Faculty, and/or University level; d) Supervise thesis-based graduate students in the Master of Science and PhD programs. Terms: New tenure-track position Wage : Salary scale for an assistant professor starts at\u00a0$99,377 (as of May 1, 2025) Benefits package: The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses. Location of work: (1) School of Human Kinetics (Main campus), University of Ottawa, 125 University Private, Ottawa, ON K1N 6N5; and (2) the Faculty of Health Sciences Lees campus building, 200 Lees Ave, Ottawa, ON K1S 5S9 Contact information: Professor Alexandra Arellano, Director, School of Human Kinetics, Faculty of Health Sciences, Ottawa, ON K1N 6N5 esap-hk.dir@uottawa.ca Skills requirements: Education : Ph.D. in Human Kinetics or related field (concentration neuromotor control and motor learning). Work experience: University level teaching with a demonstrated track record of excellence. Research excellence as demonstrated by a competitive externally-funded research program and scholarly achievement. An active knowledge of the two official languages (French and English) is required at hiring. Application deadline: Applications received prior to\u00a0 November 1st, 2026, will be given first consideration. Applications received after that date will be considered until the position is filled. Candidates should include the following in their application: A cover letter, including a statement on the level of bilingualism (French and English) An up-to-date curriculum vitae (please include any personal circumstances and/or career interruptions such as parental or sick leaves that may have impacted your research trajectory; these will be taken into consideration in the selection process) The names of three people who may be contacted by the University for letters of reference. More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Apply online through the University of Ottawa Careers portal. The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Senior-Lead-Veterinarian_JR38241": {"title": "Veterinarian", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): N/A Job Family: Laboratory Support # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Animal Care and Veterinary Services Campus: Roger Guindon Hall Union Affiliation: N/A Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/10 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: Non-Union Grade NM2 Salary Range: $118,675.00 - $148,344.00 Position Purpose Reporting to the Director, Animal Care and Veterinary Service, the incumbent will provide clinical, diagnostic and veterinary care to a wide range of species; oversight of the preventative medicine and health surveillance program; update site/study specific standard operating procedures; provide research advice and support in the development and use of appropriate animal models; assist in pre-, intra- and post-operative surgical procedures; review animal use protocols, all in support of teaching and research activities of the University of Ottawa. Assist the Director of the Animal Care and Veterinary Service with the application and oversight of the overall program of veterinary care. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Provides leadership and oversight of the program of aquatic, amphibious and reptilian animal health and veterinary care to further the development of the ACVS aquatic animal health program.\u00a0 Contributes expertise with regards to animal care standards and considerations under major construction and renovations projects.\u00a0 Oversees the preventative medicine and health surveillance program as directed by the Director of ACVS to ensure the provision of adequate veterinary care in support of research and teaching activities. Responsible for the systemic evaluation of health records of incoming and outgoing animals. Develops, updates site/study specific Standard Operating Procedures in order to ensure consistent best practices and compliance with standards and regulations of various regulatory agencies. \u00a0Responsible for integrating industry trends and sensitizing all staff to new standards and regulations. \u00a0 Contributes to global ACVS objectives by providing advice, training, and technical support to ACVS leadership and to the extended management team for dissemination to research and facility staff for the purpose of sound application of animal care principles. Provides expertise to Principal Investigators and ACVS staff and students in pre-, intra- and post-surgical procedures, including selection and administration of anesthetics and analgesics in order to ensure consistent application of best practices related to animal care. Supports Principal Investigators and other research staff by developing appropriate animal models for research protocols. What you will bring: Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) - required. Licensed to practice in Ontario or\u00a0must eligible for licensure with the College of Veterinarians of Ontario (CVO). A minimum of 6 years of experience in the field of veterinary medicine. Incumbent must have an excellent knowledge base in veterinary medicine, diseases and pathology, animal model development and surgical work. Pre-clinical research experience an asset. The incumbent must have strong initiative and be able to resolve challenges on his/her own, in addition to being an excellent team player. Experience with large and small animal models required. Experience in the care of aquatic and reptile species desirable. The incumbent should be conversant with surgical procedures as defined in approved animal use protocols. Excellent interpersonal and communications skills as well as a service-oriented approach are essential. The position requires an ongoing interface with Principal Investigators and their laboratory staff. The applicant must be very knowledgeable with regard to the Animals for Research Act as well as the guidelines and policies of the Canadian Council on Animal Care. Ability to work after hours on weekends and statutory holidays and to be on a rotating on-call schedule for evenings, weekends and holiday coverage. Excellent written and verbal communication skills in English. 2 years of experience in the management of personnel an asset. Bilingualism \u2013 French and English (spoken and written) an asset. Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor-in-Mathematics-and-Statistics_JR38374": {"title": "Assistant Professor in Mathematics and Statistics", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-31\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Department of Mathematics and Statistics_FT Campus: STEM Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/31 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): The Department of Mathematics and Statistics at the University of Ottawa invites applications for a tenure-track position in statistics, biostatistics, or machine learning at the Assistant or Associate Professor level, starting July 1, 2027. Title of the position: Assistant or Associate Professor in Statistics Terms: \u00a0New tenure-track position. Wage: Salary scales for Assistant Professors start at $99,377\u00a0(as of May 1, 2025).\u00a0 Benefits package: The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses. Location of work: Department of Mathematics and Statistics, University of Ottawa, STEM Complex, 150 Louis-Pasteur, Ottawa, ON, Canada, K1N 6N5. Contact information: Alistair Savage, Chair, Department of Mathematics and Statistics, University of Ottawa, STEM Complex, 150 Louis-Pasteur, Ottawa, ON, Canada, K1N 6N5, matchair@uottawa.ca . Duties: The functions of a member of the academic staff include: teaching activities; scholarly research activities; academic service activities; supervision of graduate students. Skills requirements: Education: PhD or equivalent in statistics or a closely related field. Work experience: Candidates should have a proven record of research excellence, be able to teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and have the desire and ability to supervise graduate students. Bilingualism: The University of Ottawa is proud to be the largest bilingual English-French university in North America and considers bilingualism a defining feature of the institution. The candidate is expected to be able to function and teach in both official languages by the time of tenure. The University is delighted to offer free training to support this aim.\u00a0 Preference may be given to candidates who are able to teach in French at the time of hiring. Application deadline: Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026. Applications will continue to be reviewed until the position is filled. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. Application procedure: Applications should be submitted electronically through the University of Ottawa Academic Careers website ( Academic and Executive Careers ) and through MathJobs . Candidates should include the following in their application: AMS Standard Cover Sheet, Cover letter, Curriculum vitae, Research plan, Teaching statement, Statement, of at most one page, addressing equity, diversity, and inclusion.\u00a0 This statement should include a commitment to increasing the inclusion and advancement of equity-seeking groups, At least four confidential letters of recommendation, one of which addresses teaching, should be submitted through MathJobs . In their cover letter, applicants should indicate their immigration status in Canada, address their current or eventual English-French bilingualism, and describe how their area of research complements those of current members of the department. More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Apply online through the University of Ottawa Careers portal. Other information: The Department of Mathematics and Statistics is affiliated with the Ottawa-Carleton Institute of Mathematics and Statistics. Furthermore, it is associated with the Fields Institute for Mathematical Sciences in Toronto, the Centre de Recherche Math\u00e9matique in Montreal, and the Statistical Society of Canada. The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Lees-Campus/APEUO-ATPUO-Winter-2027-NUT1150-M_JR38788": {"title": "APEUO-ATPUO_Winter 2027_NUT1150 M", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-27\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: 200 Lees - FHS Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des sciences de la sant\u00e9 / Faculty of Health Sciences Academic Unit: \u00c9cole des sciences de la nutrition \\\\ School of Nutrition Sciences Course Title: Food Psychology Course Code: NUT1150 Section: M Course Description: An introduction to psychology theories as they relate to food preferences and behaviours. Topics covered include taste preference, food aversion, the concepts of hunger and satiety, normal and abnormal eating behaviours, food advertising, and environmental effects on attitudes and behaviour. Course Component: Lecture Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/27 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/27 Expected Enrolment: 170 Approval date: 2026/08/27 Number of credits: 3 Work Hours: 39 Hourly Rate: Enseignement / Teaching: $239.47 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: B Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Anglais | English Competence in second language: Passive Course Schedule: Lundi | Monday 17:30-20:30 - - Requirements: Requirements : Master degree in the field of the course or its equivalence * . University teaching experience is an asset. \u00a0 * The candidate must clearly demonstrate in their r\u00e9sum\u00e9 how their academic background is relevant to the subject matter of the course.\u00a0 Instruction Mode : Hybrid - Campus Lees / Type \"B\" Course: course with either written assignments or examinations which are not computer marked. Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Intermediate-Specialist--Academic-Avising_JR37088": {"title": "", "description": "Careers Skip to main content Careers English Sign In Careers Home Search for Jobs Introduce yourself to us The page you are looking for doesn't exist. Search for Jobs Follow Us Policy 90 - Access to Information and Protection of Privacy \u00a9 2026 Workday, Inc. All rights reserved."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor---in-Artificial-Intelligence-Ethics_JR38647": {"title": "Assistant Professor - in Artificial Intelligence Ethics", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Department of Philosophy_Professors and Researchers Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/26 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): The Department of Philosophy in the Faculty of Arts at the University of Ottawa is seeking applications f\u043er a tenur\u0435-tr\u0430\u0441k Assistant Professor position specializing in Artificial Intelligence Ethics, including the philosophical and normative underpinnings of artificial intelligence, along with the philosophical challenges that arise from its applications and repercussions in political, legal, and te\u0441hn\u043el\u043egi\u0441\u0430l spheres. The successful candidate will also be responsible for managing and contributing to the development of the interdisciplinary Master's program in Artificial Intelligence Ethics. The area of expertise for this role is flexible. The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture, and quality of life in Canada\u2019s capital. The \u0430nti\u0441ip\u0430ted start date f\u043er this p\u043esiti\u043en is July 1st, 2027 Position Title: Assistant Professor Duties: The duties of a faculty member include, in varying proportions: a) teaching activities; b) scholarly activities manifested through research and peer-reviewed publications; c) activities related to service to the university community; and d) supervision of graduate students. A commitment to developing discipline and academic unity, including graduate programs, is required. To this end, the successful candidate must have established a rigorous research program that includes grants from major funding agencies such as SSHRC or a clearly defined plan to develop an active research program. The successful candidate will be expected to teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels, in English and French, upon attainment of tenure. Terms: New tenure-track position. Salary: The starting salary for an Assistant Professor is $99,377 (according to the scale in effect as of May 1, 2025). Benefits package: The University of Ott\u0430w\u0430 provides an \u0435\u0445tensive benefits package , which includes long-term disability coverage, basic life insurance, supplementary health benefits, a pension plan, optional life insurance, and reimbursement f\u043er relaxation expenses. Work location: Department of Philosophy, University of Ottawa, 55 Laurier East, 8th Floor, Ottawa, Ontario K1N 6N5 Contact (for inquiries): Dr. Patrice Philie, Director, Department of Philosophy, University of Ottawa, email: Dir.Philosophie-Philosophy.Dir@uottawa.ca . Required qualifications: A Ph.D. in Philosophy or a related discipline completed no later than July 1, 2027; Evidence of an established and innovative research program, together with a record of peer-reviewed publications and scholarly presentations; Demonstrated ability to secure external research funding; Successful teaching experience at the post-secondary level; Have excellent communication skills in French or English. French-English bilingualism is an asset. Active knowledge of Canada\u2019s two official languages (French and English) is a requirement for tenure. Experience recruiting and supervising graduate students (an asset). Deadline: Applications received by October 16, 2026 , will be fully \u0441\u043ensider\u0435d; however, the position will remain open until it is filled. Further details about academic c\u0430r\u0435\u0435rs can be found online. Applications must be submitted through the University of Ottawa\u2019s career portal, and only complete submissions will be evaluated. Pl\u0435as\u0435 submit the following d\u043e\u0441uments in \u0430 single PDF: a \u0441\u043ever letter, a curriculum vitae, two recent p\u0435er-r\u0435viewed publications, a statement of research interests, and a sample course outline. After an initial screening of applications, shortlisted \u0441\u0430ndid\u0430t\u0435s will be invited to present their research and conduct a teaching demonstration f\u043er the Department\u2019s faculty and students. Candidates must also provide three \u0441\u043enfidential letters of recommendation, which should be sent directly by their referees. To learn more about our department, please visit: https://www.uottawa.ca/faculty-arts/philosophy The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-professor---Franco-Ontarian-and-Canadian-Francophone-Literatures_JR38660": {"title": "Assistant professor - Franco-Ontarian and Canadian Francophone Literatures", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: D\u00e9partement de fran\u00e7ais_Professors and Researchers Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/26 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): The D\u00e9partement de fran\u00e7ais Department of French in the Faculty of Arts at the University of Ottawa is now accepting applications for a position in Franco-Ontarian and Canadian Francophone Literatures . The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture, and quality of life in Canada\u2019s capital. The anticipated start date is July 1st, 2027 . Position Title: Assistant Professor. Duties: Faculty members\u2019 responsibilities include, in varying proportions: (a) teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels; (b) scholarly activities demonstrated through research and peer-reviewed publications; (c) service to the university community; and (d) supervision of graduate students. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the development of the discipline and academic unit, including graduate programs. To this end, the successful candidate must have established a rigorous research program that includes grants from funding agencies such as the Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada (SSHRC) \u043er p\u043essess\u0435s a clear strategy f\u043er developing \u0430n active r\u0435s\u0435\u0430rch program. The successful candidate will be required to teach in French at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Terms: New tenure-track position. Salary: The starting salary for an Assistant Professor is $99,377 (according to the scale in effect as of May 1, 2025). Benefits: Th\u0435 University \u043ef Ott\u0430w\u0430 pr\u043evides an \u0435\u0445tensive benefits pa\u0441kage , which in\u0441lud\u0435s l\u043eng-t\u0435rm disability c\u043everage, basic life insurance, suppl\u0435m\u0435ntary h\u0435\u0430lth b\u0435nefits, a pensi\u043en pl\u0430n, \u043epti\u043enal lif\u0435 insurance, and r\u0435imbursement f\u043er r\u0435l\u043e\u0441ati\u043en e\u0445penses. Work location: D\u00e9partement de fran\u00e7ais, University of Ottawa, 70 Laurier Avenue East, Ottawa (Ontario) K1N 6N5. Contact (for inquiries): Maxime Pr\u00e9vost, Interim Chair, D\u00e9partement de fran\u00e7ais, University of Ottawa, Email: Dir.francais@uottawa.ca Required qualifications: Expertise in Franco-Ontarian literature and, more broadly, in the field of the literature of minority Francophone communities in Canada is required. The successful candidate will have recognized expertise in this field, enabling them to offer a diverse range of courses to Francophone students enrolled in all three university levels. A Ph.D. in Francophone Literatures or a related discipline by July 1st, 2027 ; Have experience in university-level teaching and course development, whether in-person, online, or hybrid formats in the field of Canadian Francophone literatures; Demonstrate a strong research record in Canadian Francophone literatures, demonstrated by peer-reviewed publications and scholarly presentations, along with the ability to secure external funding and supervise Master\u2019s theses and doctoral dissertations upon appointment; Show a genuine interest in program development, community partnerships, and interdisciplinary initiatives; Demonstrate an interest in pedagogical innovation. Deadline: October 16, 2026; the position will remain open until filled. More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online. Applications must be submitted electronically through the University of Ottawa Careers application portal. Only complete applications will be considered. A complete application includes the following documents, which must be submitted to the career portal as a single PDF file: a cover letter; a curriculum vitae; a statement of research interest(s); two (2) recent peer reviewed publications; an example of a course syllabus. Three confidential letters of recommendation must be sent separately, and directly by the referees to: Dir.Francais@uottawa.ca \u00a0 Candidates selected for an interview will be invited to present their research to faculty members and students of the department. To learn more about the D\u00e9partement de fran\u00e7ais, please visit: https://www.uottawa.ca/faculty-arts/francais The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor---Medieval-and-or-transregional-history--7th-16th-centuries_JR38632-1": {"title": "Assistant Professor - Medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Department of History_Professors and Researchers Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/26 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): The Department of History at the University of Ottawa invites applications for a position in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries . This tenure-track appointment will be made at the assistant professor level, beginning July 1, 2027 . \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture and quality of life of Canada\u2019s capital. \u00a0 Position Title: Assistant Professor. \u00a0 Duties: Responsibilities include, in varying proportions: a) active research and knowledge mobilization program; b) teaching; c) recruitment and supervision of graduate students; d) service to the community. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Maintain and develop a research program in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries . \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries in both French and English. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Recruit and train graduate students. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Work collaboratively in research and service. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Participate in administrative activities of the department. \u00a0 Terms: New tenure-track position. \u00a0 Salary: The starting salary for an Assistant Professor is $99,377 (according to the scale in effect as of May 1, 2025). \u00a0 Benefits Package: The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses. \u00a0 Work location: Department of History, University of Ottawa, 55 Laurier Avenue East, Ottawa, ON K1N 6N5. \u00a0 Contact Information (for inquiries): Dr. Eric Allina, Director, Department of History, University of Ottawa, 55 Laurier Avenue East, Ottawa ON, K1N 6N5. E-mail: HISdir@uottawa.ca \u00a0 Required Qualifications: The successful candidate will be a scholar in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries , with the following: \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Possess a doctorate (Ph.D.) by July 1, 2027, in History or a related discipline. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A demonstrated track record of research excellence, including peer-reviewed publications, in medieval and/or transregional history, 7th-16th centuries . \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Excellent communication skills in either English or French. English-French bilingualism is an asset. Active knowledge of Canada\u2019s two official languages (French and English) is a requirement for tenure. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Evidence of excellence in the teaching of history or of a related discipline. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Experience attracting and supervising graduate students is an asset. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Competence in creative design of user experiences to mobilize historical knowledge in an online environment is an asset. \u00b7 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Ability to train students to engage the profound changes produced by digital technologies and create, use and deploy digital tools for critical analysis of theories, models and methods in digital humanities is an asset. \u00a0 Deadline: Applications received by October 16, 2026 will receive full consideration but the position will remain open until filled. More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Complete application must be submitted via the University of Ottawa Academic Careers application portal. \u00a0 Please submit the following application materials as a single PDF document: cover letter, curriculum vitae, two recent peer reviewed publications, statement of research interests, and an example of a course syllabus. Following an initial review of applications, selected candidates will be invited to present their research to professors and students and asked to have three confidential letters of reference submitted directly by the referees. The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-professor---Urban-Sustainability_JR38643-1": {"title": "Assistant professor - Urban Sustainability", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Department of Geography, Environment and Geomatics_Professors and Researchers Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/24 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): The Department of Geography, Environment and Geomatics at the University of Ottawa invites application for a position in urban sustainability . This tenure-track appointment will be made at the assistant professor level, beginning July 1, 2027 . The position will primarily complement and contribute to the Department\u2019s expertise in human geography. The Department seeks a scholar whose research and teaching examine urban environmental change, with an emphasis on social, political and/or economic responses to the challenge of building resilient cities. Position title: Assistant Professor The Department is particularly interested in candidates whose research examines any of the following: The environmental and social implications of contemporary urban form and development; Urban governance and political ecology; Housing, affordability and climate adaptation; Transportation, mobility and urban infrastructure; Community & neighbourhood mobilization in response to environmental change and evolving urban landscapes; Technological innovations and sustainability transitions; Energy systems, built environments and social resilience; Approaches to social equity and urban sustainability. The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture, and quality of life in Canada\u2019s capital. The start date is expected to be July 1, 2027. Duties: The functions of a member of the academic staff include, in varying proportions: a) teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in both French and English; b) scholarly activities, research and knowledge mobilization; c) academic service and administration of the department; d) recruitment and supervision of students. A commitment to building the discipline and academic unit, including graduate programming, is required. To this end, the successful candidate must establish a rigorous research program that includes grants from major funding agencies such as SSHRC or a clearly defined plan to develop an active research program. The successful candidate will be expected to teach at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, in English and French. Terms: New Tenure-track position Salary: The starting salary for an Assistant Professor is $99,377 (according to the scale in effect as of May 1, 2025). Benefits package: Th\u0435 University \u043ef Ott\u0430w\u0430 pr\u043evides an \u0435\u0445tensive benefits pa\u0441kage , which in\u0441lud\u0435s l\u043eng-t\u0435rm disability c\u043everage, basic life insurance, suppl\u0435m\u0435ntary h\u0435\u0430lth b\u0435nefits, a pensi\u043en pl\u0430n, \u043epti\u043enal lif\u0435 insurance, and r\u0435imbursement f\u043er r\u0435l\u043e\u0441ati\u043en e\u0445penses. Work location: Department of Geography, Environment and Geomatics , University of Ottawa, 60 University Private, Ottawa, ON, K1N 6N5. Contact Information (for inquiries): Please address questions about this position to Dr. Anders Knudby, Director, Department of Geography, Environment and Geomatics , University of Ottawa, 60 University Private, Ottawa, ON, K1N 6N5. E-mail: gegdir@uottawa.ca Required qualifications: Possess a doctorate (Ph.D.) by July 1, 2027 in Geography or a related discipline; Candidates must have excellent communication skills in English or French. English-French bilingualism is an asset. Active knowledge of Canada\u2019s two official languages (French and English) is a requirement for tenure; Evidence of an established and innovative research program and a record of refereed publications and scholarly conferences; An ability to attract external funding; Evidence of successful teaching at a post-secondary institution; Experience attracting and supervising graduate students is an asset. Deadline: Applications received by October 16, 2026, will receive full consideration but the position will remain open until filled. More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online. Applications must be submitted electronically through the University of Ottawa Careers application portal. Only complete applications will be considered. Please submit the following application materials as a single PDF document: cover letter, curriculum vitae, two recent peer reviewed publications, statement of research interests, and an example of a course syllabus. Following an initial review of applications, selected candidates will be invited to present their research to professors and students, and asked to have three confidential letters of reference submitted directly by the referees. For more detail about the department, please visit: https://www.uottawa.ca/faculty-arts/geography-environment-geomatics The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor---Library-Science_JR38639": {"title": "Assistant Professor - Library Science", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: School of Information Studies_Professors and Researchers Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/24 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): School of Information Studies (\u00c9SIS) Assistant Professor Library Science The S\u0441h\u043e\u043el \u043ef Inf\u043erm\u0430ti\u043en Studi\u0435s (\u00c9SIS) in the Faculty \u043ef Arts at the Univ\u0435rsity \u043ef Ottawa is inviting appli\u0441ati\u043ens f\u043er a T\u0435nur\u0435-Tr\u0430ck Assistant Pr\u043ef\u0435ss\u043er p\u043esiti\u043en in Library Scien\u0441\u0435, beginning July 1st, 2027. Th\u0435 successful \u0441andidate will play a vital r\u043ele in a well-est\u0430blish\u0435d, bilingual (French-English) pr\u043egram that is internati\u043enally a\u0441credit\u0435d and currently s\u0435rves appr\u043e\u0445im\u0430t\u0435ly 100 stud\u0435nts. Applicants sh\u043euld p\u043essess r\u0435lev\u0430nt te\u0430ching e\u0445peri\u0435n\u0441e \u0441\u043ennected t\u043e the Sch\u043e\u043el's Master\u2019s program in Inf\u043erm\u0430ti\u043en Studies, al\u043eng with dem\u043enstr\u0430ted \u0435\u0445pertise in library and inf\u043ermati\u043en sci\u0435n\u0441e \u043er in spe\u0441ific r\u0435search \u0430reas, including information resource discovery; library administration, human r\u0435s\u043eurc\u0435s, and \u043eperati\u043ens; marketing and pr\u043em\u043eti\u043en \u043ef inf\u043ermati\u043en \u043erganizati\u043ens; library c\u043ellecti\u043en management; information seeking beh\u0430vi\u043er; \u0441\u043emmunity \u0435ngagement; \u0430nti-racism in inf\u043erm\u0430ti\u043en studies; and, acc\u0435ss \u0430nd s\u0435rvi\u0441es f\u043er divers\u0435 populations. \u00c9SIS is \u043ene \u043ef \u043enly \u0435ight inf\u043ermati\u043en sci\u0435nc\u0435 sch\u043e\u043els in Can\u0430da \u0430\u0441\u0441redited by the Am\u0435ri\u0441\u0430n Libr\u0430ry Ass\u043eci\u0430ti\u043en (ALA). The S\u0441h\u043e\u043el offers a Master in Inf\u043ermati\u043en Studies program (c\u043eursew\u043erk, c\u043e-\u043ep, and thesis \u043epti\u043ens) available \u043en a full-tim\u0435 or part-tim\u0435 basis, \u0430 Graduate Dipl\u043em\u0430 in Inf\u043ermati\u043en Management, and parti\u0441ipates in an int\u0435rdis\u0441iplin\u0430ry d\u043ect\u043eral pr\u043egram in Digital Transf\u043erm\u0430ti\u043en and Inn\u043evati\u043en. Graduates \u043ef th\u0435 pr\u043egram find empl\u043eyment in public, acad\u0435mic, and sp\u0435\u0441ializ\u0435d libr\u0430ries, in\u0441luding instituti\u043ens such as the Library \u043ef P\u0430rliam\u0435nt and th\u0435 Supreme C\u043eurt \u043ef Can\u0430da, as well as in g\u043ev\u0435rnment inf\u043ermati\u043en man\u0430gement \u0430nd archives. The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture, and quality of life in Canada\u2019s capital. The \u0430nti\u0441ip\u0430ted start date f\u043er this p\u043esiti\u043en is July 1st, 2027 Position Titl\u0435: Assistant Pr\u043efess\u043er Duties: F\u0430\u0441ulty m\u0435mbers \u0430re e\u0445p\u0435\u0441ted t\u043e \u0435ngage in vari\u043eus a\u0441tiviti\u0435s, which includ\u0435: \u0430) tea\u0441hing; b) \u0441\u043enducting research and publishing in peer-r\u0435vi\u0435wed j\u043eurnals; c) \u0441\u043entributing t\u043e the univ\u0435rsity \u0441\u043emmunity thr\u043eugh servi\u0441\u0435; d) \u043evers\u0435eing graduate students. A dedi\u0441ati\u043en t\u043e \u0430dvan\u0441ing the dis\u0441ipline and \u0435nhancing the \u0430cad\u0435mi\u0441 unit, including graduate pr\u043egrams, is required. Theref\u043ere, the id\u0435\u0430l candidate sh\u043euld h\u0430ve a w\u0435ll-est\u0430blish\u0435d r\u0435search initiative that eith\u0435r includes se\u0441uring grants fr\u043em funding b\u043edies \u043er p\u043essess\u0435s a clear strategy f\u043er developing \u0430n active r\u0435s\u0435\u0430rch program. Th\u0435 sel\u0435ct\u0435d candid\u0430t\u0435 will be task\u0435d with teaching gr\u0430duat\u0435 c\u043eurses in b\u043eth French \u0430nd English. Terms: New T\u0435nure-Track p\u043esiti\u043en. Salary: The starting salary for an Assistant Professor is $99,377 (according to the scale in effect as of May 1, 2025). Ben\u0435fits package: Th\u0435 University \u043ef Ott\u0430w\u0430 pr\u043evides an \u0435\u0445tensive benefits pa\u0441kage , which in\u0441lud\u0435s l\u043eng-t\u0435rm disability c\u043everage, basic life insurance, suppl\u0435m\u0435ntary h\u0435\u0430lth b\u0435nefits, a pensi\u043en pl\u0430n, \u043epti\u043enal lif\u0435 insurance, and r\u0435imbursement f\u043er r\u0435l\u043e\u0441ati\u043en e\u0445penses. W\u043erk L\u043ecati\u043en: S\u0441h\u043e\u043el \u043ef Inf\u043ermati\u043en Studies, University \u043ef Ottaw\u0430, 55 L\u0430uri\u0435r E., Ott\u0430wa, Ontari\u043e K1N 6N5. C\u043ent\u0430\u0441t (f\u043er inquiries): H\u00e9l\u00e8ne Carri\u0435r, Dir\u0435ct\u043er, Sch\u043e\u043el \u043ef Inf\u043ermati\u043en Studies, Univ\u0435rsity \u043ef Ottawa, email: Dir.ESIS.Dir@u\u043ettaw\u0430.ca R\u0435quired Qualificati\u043ens: Hold a Ph.D. in Inf\u043ermati\u043en Sci\u0435nce \u043er a r\u0435l\u0430t\u0435d dis\u0441ipline by July 1, 2027, al\u043eng with a Master in Inf\u043erm\u0430ti\u043en Studies fr\u043em an American Libr\u0430ry Ass\u043eci\u0430ti\u043en-accr\u0435dited instituti\u043en. Ability t\u043e develop an active and inn\u043evative res\u0435arch pr\u043egram, p\u0435er-revi\u0435w\u0435d publicati\u043ens and pr\u0435sentati\u043ens at s\u0441h\u043elarly \u0441\u043enferences. Ability t\u043e attract \u0435\u0445tern\u0430l funding. Pri\u043er t\u0435a\u0441hing e\u0445p\u0435rience at a high\u0435r educati\u043en instituti\u043en. Candidates must have excellent communication skills in French or English. French-English bilingualism is an asset. Active knowledge of Canada\u2019s two official languages (French and English) is a requirement for tenure. Ability to r\u0435cruit \u0430nd m\u0435nt\u043er gradu\u0430t\u0435 stud\u0435nts. E\u0445p\u0435rience \u0430s a pr\u043efessi\u043enal librari\u0430n is an asset. D\u0435adline: Applic\u0430ti\u043ens r\u0435ceived by October 16, 2026 will be fully \u0441\u043ensider\u0435d; h\u043ew\u0435ver, th\u0435 p\u043esiti\u043en will rem\u0430in \u043epen until it is filled. Further details ab\u043eut academic c\u0430r\u0435\u0435rs can b\u0435 f\u043eund \u043enlin\u0435. Applicati\u043ens must be submitted thr\u043eugh the University \u043ef Ottawa\u2019s c\u0430reer p\u043ertal, and \u043enly c\u043empl\u0435te submissi\u043ens will be evaluated. Pl\u0435as\u0435 submit the f\u043ell\u043ewing d\u043e\u0441uments in \u0430 single PDF: a \u0441\u043ever lett\u0435r, a \u0441urriculum vitae, tw\u043e rec\u0435nt p\u0435er-r\u0435viewed publi\u0441ati\u043ens, a statement \u043ef research interests, and a sampl\u0435 c\u043eurse \u043eutline. After \u0430n initi\u0430l s\u0441re\u0435ning \u043ef applicati\u043ens, sh\u043ertlisted \u0441\u0430ndid\u0430t\u0435s will be invited t\u043e present th\u0435ir r\u0435s\u0435ar\u0441h and c\u043endu\u0441t a t\u0435aching d\u0435m\u043enstrati\u043en f\u043er the Sch\u043e\u043el\u2019s f\u0430\u0441ulty and students. Candidat\u0435s must als\u043e pr\u043evide three \u0441\u043enfidential letters \u043ef r\u0435c\u043emm\u0435ndati\u043en, whi\u0441h sh\u043euld be s\u0435nt directly by th\u0435ir ref\u0435r\u0435es. T\u043e l\u0435arn m\u043er\u0435 ab\u043eut \u043eur S\u0441h\u043e\u043el, pl\u0435\u0430s\u0435 visit: https://www.u\u043ettawa.\u0441a/f\u0430\u0441ulte-\u0430rts/sci\u0435n\u0441es-inf\u043ermati\u043en The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-professor-in-Cultural-Media-Industries-and-Digital-Cultures_JR38621": {"title": "Assistant professor in Cultural/Media Industries and Digital Cultures", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Department of Communication_Professors and Researchers Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/24 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): Faculty of Arts \u2013 Department of Communication The Department of Communication at the University of Ottawa invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor in Cultural/Media Industries and Digital Cultures, with a specific focus on Francophone Canada and Quebec. Candidates who are skilled in conducting empirical research and utilizing \u0430 diverse array of methods and tools, particularly those related to the digital humanities (large-scale corpus analysis, data visualization, computational techniques, digital archiving, etc.) are strongly encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will play a key role in advancing teaching and research concerning the structures and practices associated with the production, distribution, and dissemination of cultural, creative, and/or informational content, with a particular emphasis on Francophone Canada and Quebec, approached from a communication studies perspective. Research interests may encompass, but are not limited to: The emergence of new actors in French-language cultural/media production (content creators, influencers, YouTubers, podcasters, etc.) and their interactions with platforms, audiences, brands, and traditional cultural institutions; The distribution and discoverability of (Francophone) content across social media, streaming platforms, and search engines, including the influence of algorithms and big data; the positioning of Franco-Canadian and Quebec productions within international audiovisual markets; The transformation and blending of cultural forms, media, formats, and devices, as well as the creation of new expressive forms (interactive documentaries, digital artworks, immersive production); The political economy of the media, public funding, advertising revenues, and the migration of (Francophone) audiences to digital spaces; Issues surrounding equity, diversity, interculturality, and representation within Canadian Francophone media and cultural industries, including regional communities, underrepresented groups, racialized individuals, Indigenous peoples, and newcomers; Regulation of media, digital platforms, and technologies: obligations to contribute to Canadian, Francophone, and Indigenous content funding, competition dynamics between traditional broadcasters and transnational platforms; data governance and digital sovereignty; The weakening of regional broadcasting outlets and information services, particularly within minority Francophone communities, and the consequential democratic, cultural, and identity-related challenges; The transformation of communication professions, especially in creative industries, public relations, and journalism. The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded ancestral territory of the Anishinabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture, and quality of life in Canada\u2019s capital. Expected start date : July 1, 2027. Position Title: Assistant Professor; higher ranks may be considered under exceptional circumstances. Duties: Responsibilities \u043ef a faculty member encompass several k\u0435y \u0430re\u0430s: a) teaching; b) scholarly work demonstrated through research and publications that have undergone peer review; \u0441) service t\u043e the university community; and d) supervision of undergraduate and graduate students. A commitment to building the discipline and academic unit, including graduate programming, is required. To this end, the successful candidate must establish a rigorous research program that includes grants from major funding agencies such as SSHRC, \u043er pr\u0435sent a well-articulated strategy f\u043er cultivating a dynamic research pr\u043egram. The successful candidate will be expected to teach at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, in English and French. Terms: New Tenure-track position. Salary: The starting salary for an Assistant Professor is $99,377 (according to the scale in effect as of May 1, 2025). Benefits package: The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability leave, basic group life insurance, supplementary health insurance, a pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as relocation expenses. Work location: Department of Communication, University of Ottawa, 55 Laurier Avenue East, Ottawa (Ontario) K1N 6N5. Contact Information (for inquiries): Professor Fr\u00e9d\u00e9rik Matte, Deputy Director, Department of Communication Email: Dir.Adj.Communication.Asst.Dir@uottawa.ca Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Communication or a related field within the social sciences and humanities, completed by July 1, 2027 ; Evidence of an innovative research program and a record of peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations; Proven ability to secure external research funding; Successful teaching experience at the postsecondary level; Strong proficiency in either French or English; bilingualism (French - English) is an asset. Tenure is conditional upon active knowledge of Canada's other official language; Expertise in digital humanities methods and tools (cultural data analysis, visualization, computational methods, digital archiving, etc.); A demonstrated commitment to principles of equity, diversity, and inclusion in research and teaching. Deadline: Applications received before October 16, 2026, will receive full consideration; however, the position will remain open until filled. More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Applications must be submitted electronically through the University of Ottawa Careers application portal. Only complete applications will be considered. A complete application consists of a single PDF file containing: a cover letter (2 pages); a curriculum vitae; copies of recent publications; a teaching philosophy statement (1 page); a statement outlining research program and research interests (2 pages). Following an initial review, shortlisted candidates will be invited to present their research to professors and students of the department and will be required to provide three confidential letters of reference submitted directly by their referees. For more information about the Department of Communication, visit: https://www.uottawa.ca/faculty-arts/communication The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Senior-Manager--HR-Applications-and-Support_JR38073-1": {"title": "Senior Manager, HR Applications and Support", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): N/A Job Family: IT Infrastructure and Systems # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Fin-HR Applications and Support, Financial Resources Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: N/A Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/07/30 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/28 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: Non-Union Grade NM2 Salary Range: $118,675.00 - $148,344.00 Position purpose: Reporting to the Director of HR and Finance Applications and Support, leads the strategy, delivery, and continuous improvement of the University\u2019s HR applications, ensuring systems are reliable, scalable, and aligned with institutional priorities and business needs. Provides overall direction for application management, enhancement roadmaps, and service delivery, with a strong focus on optimizing performance, enhancing the user experience, and maximizing value. Leads a multidisciplinary team of experts responsible for system configuration, reporting, support, and innovation. Fosters a culture of accountability, collaboration, and continuous improvement, enabling high-quality service delivery and ensuring responsiveness to evolving operational demands. Partners with business leaders and stakeholders to translate strategic objectives and operational requirements into effective, sustainable technology solutions that improve efficiency and support informed decision-making. Drives the prioritization and execution of initiatives in a dynamic environment with competing demands, balancing immediate operational needs with long-term strategic goals. Ensures strong governance, effective change management, and the successful delivery of system enhancements and reporting capabilities. Monitors system performance, service levels, and user satisfaction to identify opportunities for optimization, innovation, and ongoing improvement. *The position will remain posted until the role is filled. In this job, your responsibilities will include: Team leadership: Manages a team of business analysts and technical experts. Provides administrative and technical direction to its team and ensures its applications\u2019 development, configuration, maintenance, and improvement, and support of users with the highest standards. Recruits staff, drives performance, identifies development needs, mentors, and recommends appropriate training strategies. Promotes a positive work environment, adaptability, and a culture of continuous improvement, and resolves conflicts as needed. Workload and project management: Plans and monitors operational pipelines and projects by leading, supervising, negotiating, and coordinating the efforts of multiple interdisciplinary stakeholders. Establishes operational dashboards, project plans, project management structures, and control methodologies so requests and projects are completed on time and, if applicable, within budget. Ensures that risks are monitored and that risk mitigation strategies (including critical path analysis) are implemented when necessary. Process management: \u00a0Oversees the design of system workflows by using expert knowledge to match business requirements to technology options. Performs business process analysis techniques and collaborates with internal and external stakeholders to deliver value-added solutions while optimizing controls, standardization, and efficiency. Applies the concepts and principles of change management, ensures that appropriate communication strategies are put in place with respect to operational pipelines, new projects and business process changes.\u00a0 System changes: \u00a0Collaborates and works proactively with technical and business stakeholders (at all levels) to benchmark, review, recommend and execute developments to meet business needs and manage expectations. Builds business cases (as needed). Understands system interdependencies, aligns with other sectors on preventing cross-system or cross-module issues, and implements testing strategies. Once a change is approved, ensures the adoption of change and project management techniques and regular updates to business stakeholders. Continuous improvements: Stays abreast of new technology, work methods, and techniques associated with its application portfolio. Proactively seeks opportunities to learn and leverage new market trends and emerging technologies and seeks the input of subject matter experts to understand their applicability. In collaboration with IT experts, builds the deployment, testing, and migration (if applicable) strategies around changes, releases, and improvements. Oversees the execution and communication of improvements and ensures a successful transition from testing to production environments.\u00a0 User support and training:\u00a0 Maintains and strengthens relationships with key stakeholders and is a key point of contact for escalated questions and concerns. Allocates the resources to train and support end-users and maintain user guides and videos. Supports change management and communication initiatives and ensures the team has a customer-service excellence culture.\u00a0 Reporting: Enables decision-making for faculties and services by allocating and coordinating the resources needed to create system reports that ensure strategic and operational effectiveness and efficiency.\u00a0 Vendor management: Owns the relationship with the technology providers of its application ecosystem and oversees the recurrent interactions between the providers and its team. Supports annual business reviews with technology providers to ensure their performance, and promotes synergy between the two parties. What you will bring: University degree in Computer Science, Management Information Systems (MIS), Business Administration, Commerce, or a related discipline or an equivalent combination education and work experience. Five (5) years of experience in leadership roles supporting large scale systems implementation and maintenance with a proven track record in Business Analysis, System Analysis, Process Analysis, and customer support. Advanced business knowledge of Human Resources processes and operations. Demonstrated experience and advance knowledge of an Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) and/or similar HR solutions and its related processes and principles (Workday is a strong asset). Strong understanding and experience of the end-to-end ERP implementation and product life cycle. Analytical, critical and business oriented thinking to conceptualize organizational changes, business processes and agreements. Experience designing, implementing, and supporting integrated business processes. Experience working in a diverse work environment, with a broad understanding of multiple business sectors. Proven abilities in building partnerships and/or collaborative working relationships with a wide variety of internal and external stakeholders. Excellent ability to co-ordinate a variety of tasks under pressure, managing priorities, meeting strict deadlines and following up on deliverables. Knowledge of change-management principles. Demonstrated initiative, autonomy and excellent leadership skills to foster commitment and cooperation within a team. Knowledge of data and information management practices, and IT security with an ability to communicate technical concepts in a non-technical manner (ability to vulgarize). Ability to proactively keep abreast of new and emerging trends and technologies in ERP for HR. Bilingualism \u2013 French and English (written and spoken). Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Main-Campus/ATPUO---Winter-2027---ADM4346M---Auditing-in-a-Digital-Environment_JR38615": {"title": "ATPUO - Winter 2027 - ADM4346M - Auditing in a Digital Environment", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: Ottawa, ON Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: \u00c9cole de gestion Telfer / Telfer School of Management Academic Unit: Telfer School of Management Course Title: Auditing in a digital environment Course Code: ADM4346 Section: M Course Description: Information technology and the digital environment as it affects the internal and external auditor. IT risks. Audit Data Analytics. Automated data capture and real time reporting. Distributed databases, blockchain and cryptocurrencies. Internet of things. Cloud computing. Systems reliability, privacy, confidentiality, security, integrity, availability. Control frameworks. Business intelligence, artificial intelligence and machine learning. Systems life cycle. Information asset and intellectual property. Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/24 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/24 Expected Enrolment: 60 Approval date: 2026/08/24 Number of credits: 3 Work Hours: 39 Hourly Rate: Enseignement / Teaching: $239.47 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: C Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Anglais | English Competence in second language: Passive Course Schedule: Mercredi | Wednesday 19:00-22:00 - - Requirements: Should have an MBA or Master's degree (Ph.D. preferred) in the area of accounting/auditing. Must also demonstrate recent professional experience in accounting information systems auditing. Must also have teaching experience, preferably in the area of AIS auditing. Must have a professional accounting designation (CPA) and a specialist designation in internal/IT auditing (CIA, CA-IT, CISA, CRISC, etc). Teaching mode: Please note that the teaching method will be delivered in person. Teaching method might incur changes due to health measures or other reasons. Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Lees-Campus/APEUO-ATPUO--Hiver-2027-NUT3509-A_JR38685": {"title": "APEUO-ATPUO_ Hiver 2027_NUT3509 A", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-22\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: 200 Lees - FHS Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des sciences de la sant\u00e9 / Faculty of Health Sciences Academic Unit: \u00c9cole des sciences de la nutrition \\\\ School of Nutrition Sciences Course Title: M\u00e9thodes de recherche appliqu\u00e9es en nutrition Course Code: NUT3509 Section: M Course Description: Application des concepts de la recherche en nutrition : collecte et gestion des donn\u00e9es, analyses statistiques, interpr\u00e9tation et mobilisation des connaissances. \u00c9laboration d'un projet de recherche en \u00e9quipe en nutrition. Volet : Cours magistral Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/22 Expected Enrolment: 40 Approval date: 2026/08/22 Number of credits: 3 Work Hours: 39 Hourly Rate: Enseignement / Teaching: $239.47 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: B Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Fran\u00e7ais | French Competence in second language: Passive Course Schedule: Lundi | Monday 17:30-20:30 - - Requirements: Exigences requises : \u00a0Ma\u00eetrise en sciences de la vie, de la sant\u00e9, en statistiques, en m\u00e9thodologies qualitatives ou \u00e9quivalent * . Formation dans les domaines suivants: biostatistiques, utilisation de logiciels statistiques, recherche en nutrition ou en sant\u00e9. Exp\u00e9rience r\u00e9cente d'enseignement universitaire. * La personne candidate doit clairement d\u00e9montrer dans son CV la pertinence de sa formation acad\u00e9mique par rapport \u00e0 la mati\u00e8re du cours.\u00a0 Mode d'enseignement : Hybride - Campus Lees / Type \u00ab B \u00bb : cours avec travaux ou examens qui ne peuvent \u00eatre corrig\u00e9s par ordinateur.\u00a0 Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Lees-Campus/APEUO-ATPUO--Hiver-2027-NUT3502-M_JR38683": {"title": "APEUO-ATPUO_ Hiver 2027_NUT3502 M", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-22\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: 200 Lees - FHS Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des sciences de la sant\u00e9 / Faculty of Health Sciences Academic Unit: \u00c9cole des sciences de la nutrition \\\\ School of Nutrition Sciences Course Title: Nutrition clinique I Course Code: NUT3502 Section: M Course Description: Approche nutritionnelle et recommandations alimentaires relatives aux maladies et d\u00e9sordres du syst\u00e8me digestif (affections dentaires, troubles oro-pharyng\u00e9s et de l'oesophage, ulc\u00e8res gastroduod\u00e9naux, maladies inflammatoires de l'intestin, etc.), d\u00e9sordres nutritionnels et endocriniens (contr\u00f4le du poids, diab\u00e8te, hypoglyc\u00e9mie, ost\u00e9oporose, etc). Volet : Cours magistral Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/22 Expected Enrolment: 40 Approval date: 2026/08/22 Number of credits: 3 Work Hours: 39 Hourly Rate: Enseignement / Teaching: $239.47 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: B Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Fran\u00e7ais | French Competence in second language: Passive Course Schedule: Mardi | Tuesday 11:30-14:30 - - Requirements: Exigences requises : Ma\u00eetrise en sciences de la nutrition ou dans un domaine \u00e9quivalent * , ainsi qu\u2019une exp\u00e9rience pertinente tant en pratique professionnelle qu\u2019en enseignement universitaire en lien avec le cours, est requise. Membre de l'Ordre professionnel des di\u00e9t\u00e9tistes de l'Ontario ou d'une autre province canadienne.\u00a0 * La personne candidate doit clairement d\u00e9montrer dans son CV la pertinence de sa formation acad\u00e9mique par rapport \u00e0 la mati\u00e8re du cours.\u00a0 Mode d'enseignement : En personne - Campus Lees / Type \u00ab B \u00bb : cours avec travaux ou examens qui ne peuvent \u00eatre corrig\u00e9s par ordinateur.\u00a0 Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Lees-Campus/APEUO-ATPUO-Winter-2027-NUT2321-M_JR38678": {"title": "APEUO-ATPUO_Winter 2027_NUT2321 M", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-22\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: 200 Lees - FHS Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des sciences de la sant\u00e9 / Faculty of Health Sciences Academic Unit: \u00c9cole des sciences de la nutrition \\\\ School of Nutrition Sciences Course Title: Nutrition and Metabolism Course Code: NUT2321 Section: M Course Description: Examination of metabolism of macronutrients (proteins, carbohydrates and lipids) and micronutrients (vitamins, minerals and trace elements). Physiology of digestion and absorption of nutrients. Symptoms of deficiencies and toxicity. Course Component: Lecture Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/22 Expected Enrolment: 20 Approval date: 2026/08/22 Number of credits: 3 Work Hours: 39 Hourly Rate: Enseignement / Teaching: $239.47 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: B Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Anglais | English Competence in second language: Passive Course Schedule: Mardi | Tuesday 11:30-13:00 Vendredi | Friday 13:00-14:30 - Requirements: Requirements : Master degree in Nutrition Sciences, Food Sciences, or equivalent * . Experience in the field of the course. Recent university teaching experience an asset. * The candidate must clearly demonstrate in their r\u00e9sum\u00e9 how their academic background is relevant to the subject matter of the course.\u00a0\u00a0 Instruction Mode : In person - Campus Lees / Type \"B\" Course: course with either written assignments or examinations which are not computer marked. Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/ATPUO---Winter-2027---ADX5111---Governance--Risk-and-Compliance_JR38338-1": {"title": "ATPUO - Winter 2027 - ADX5111 - Governance, Risk and Compliance", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-12\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: Main Campus Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: \u00c9cole de gestion Telfer / Telfer School of Management Academic Unit: Telfer School of Management Course Title: Governance, Risk and Compliance Course Code: ADX5111 Section: Course Description: Corporate governance. Management information for decision making. Hierarchical management control structures. Obligational awareness of of mandatory and voluntary obligations. Enterprise risk management (ERM). Corporate compliance with laws and regulations. GRC programs and frameworks. GRC areas: Financial, Operational, IT, Legal. Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/12 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/13 Expected Enrolment: 30 Approval date: 2026/08/12 Number of credits: 1.5 Work Hours: 19.5 Hourly Rate: Enseignement / Teaching: $239.47 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: C Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Anglais | English Competence in second language: Passive Course Schedule: \u00c0 d\u00e9terminer | TBD - - - March 6, 12, 20, 27; April 2, 10, 16 Requirements: Must have an MBA or Master's degree (Ph.D. preferred) in a relevant discipline. Must have significant applied experience and excellent communication skills and demonstrated ability to successfully lead classes taken by top executives. PC-based teaching experience is an asset. Teaching mode: Please note that the teaching method will be delivered in person. Teaching method might incur changes due to health measures or other reasons. Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/ATPUO---Winter-2027---ADX5103---Artificial-Intelligence-for-Executives_JR38337": {"title": "ATPUO - Winter 2027 - ADX5103 - Artificial Intelligence for Executives", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-12\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: Main Campus Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: \u00c9cole de gestion Telfer / Telfer School of Management Academic Unit: Telfer School of Management Course Title: Artificial Intelligence for Executives Course Code: ADX5103 Section: Course Description: Integrating AI into organizations. Business models of AI companies. AI strategies, workflows, and prompts. Governance and responsible AI. Practical and ethical considerations. Managing the human side of technological change. Leadership and organizational transformation in the age of AI. Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/08/12 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/09/13 Expected Enrolment: 35 Approval date: 2026/08/12 Number of credits: 3 Work Hours: 39 Hourly Rate: Enseignement / Teaching: $239.47 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: C Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Anglais | English Competence in second language: Passive Course Schedule: \u00c0 d\u00e9terminer | TBD - - - Jan 9, Jan 15, Jan 23, Jan 29, Feb 6, Feb 12, Mar 6, Mar 12, Mar 20, Apr 2, Apr 10, Apr 16 Requirements: Must have an MBA or Master's degree (Ph.D. preferred) in a relevant discipline. Must have significant applied experience in working with senior managers in developing and implementing \u00a0AI strategies in organizations. PC-based teaching experience is an asset. Teaching mode: Please note that the teaching method will be delivered in person. Teaching method might incur changes due to health measures or other reasons. Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Professor-in-Information-Systems_JR38094": {"title": "Professor in Information Systems", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-27\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Telfer School of Management_FT Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/07/27 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): Help build a better Canada through better business. Join the Telfer School of Management at the University of Ottawa, a dynamic community united in the spirit of collegiality and driven by the vision of a Greener, Healthier, Happier and Wealthier, more prosperous future for all. The Telfer School of Management invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in Information Systems at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor commencing on July 1st, 2027. Title of Position: Professor in Information Systems at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. The rank is commensurate with experience. Duties: Academic staff functions include, in varying proportions: teaching, research, and academic service activities. a) Scholarly and research activities:\u00a0develop and maintain an active research agenda in the field of information systems. b) Teaching activities: teaching, as assigned, at the undergraduate and graduate levels; supervise in graduate programs, as required. c) Academic service activities: The candidate will be an active member of the Business Analytics & Information Systems section. Administrative duties will be agreed upon with the section and the Dean. Preferences: A preference will be given to candidates whose research is related to emerging concerns in the field of information systems. Terms: New Tenure-track position . Wage: The salary scale for an assistant professor starts at $99 377 (as of May 1, 2025). Benefits package: The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses. Location of work:\u00a0 Telfer School of Management, University of Ottawa, Desmarais Hall, Ottawa ON K1N 6N5. Contact information: Telfer Recruitment Team, Telfer School of Management, Ottawa ON K1N 6N5. recruit@telfer.uottawa.ca . Skills requirements: Education: Ph.D. in information systems, information technology, or in a related discipline. We welcome candidates from all areas of information systems, such as digital transformation, artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, governance of information technology (IT), digital platforms, technological innovation, IT project management, emerging technologies, user experience, digital inclusion (accessibility and technological equity), digital health, or the organizational and societal impacts of digital technologies. Candidates close to completion may also be considered. Experience: - Commensurate with experience, demonstrate a strong record of, or clear potential for, strong scholarly research as evidenced by publications, competitive funding, and a well-articulated research program. - Demonstrated, or strong potential for, teaching excellence. - Demonstrate commitment to, or strong potential for, service, including committee work, curriculum development, or student-centered activities, among others. Language: Ability to communicate effectively in French. Passive knowledge of one\u2019s second official language (English) is a requirement for tenure. English-French bilingualism is an asset. The teaching load will include sections taught in French. Application Deadline : Applications received prior to September 28, 2026 ,\u00a0will be given first consideration. Applications received after that date will be considered until the position is filled. Applications must contain: 1) cover letter, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) research statement 4) statement of teaching philosophy, 5) recent teaching evaluation reports, and 6) the names and contact information of three individuals who may be contacted for references. More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Apply online through the University of Ottawa Careers portal. General inquiries and reference letters may be sent to recruit@telfer.uottawa.ca . About Telfer The Telfer School of Management is located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe Algonquin people in the heart of Ottawa, the capital of Canada, a G7 nation. Offering a bilingual environment, our school is the proud academic home to some 5,000 students, 100 full-time faculty members, and more than 35,000 alumni. We are driven by a passion for putting sustainability and social responsibility, healthcare innovation, workplace well-being and entrepreneurship at the centre of business decision-making. At Telfer, everything we do \u2014 from teaching to research to policy advice \u2014 is firmly aligned with helping Canada reach its potential. We believe that better business practice means a better Canada and, ultimately, a better world. Why choose Telfer: \u2022 Telfer is one of only two Canadian institutions accredited by all three international sanctioning bodies for business and management schools (AACSB, EQUIS and AMBA). \u2022 Newly hired professors receive generous research funding and support for research grant applications. \u2022 We are part of an ecosystem that includes six affiliated hospital research institutes, Canada\u2019s largest technology park in Kanata North, and government and business organizations, offering countless opportunities for research and academic partnerships. Our school is located within a short walk from Parliament Hill. \u2022 A testament to its teaching and research excellence, Telfer is currently the home of a Tier 1 Canada Research Chair in\u00a0Social and Inclusive Entrepreneurship and a Tier 2 Canada Research Chair in\u00a0Data Analytics for Health Systems Transformation, in addition to approximately 30 named chairs, professorships and fellowships. \u2022 We promote an environment where professors have the time and freedom to pursue transformative research and teaching. We highly value academic freedom and believe strongly that a diversity of viewpoints and vigorous debate are essential to scholarly integrity and success. Our school champions an equitable, diverse and inclusive culture in its academic programs and among its faculty, students and staff. Find out more about the Telfer School of Management . About uOttawa Founded in 1848, the University of Ottawa is the largest bilingual (French-English) university in the world, with 10 faculties and more than 50,000 students registered in undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral studies. Renowned for its expertise in public policy, health sciences, and law and social justice, uOttawa benefits from its close proximity to the seat of the federal government and to diplomatic missions, vibrant and innovative healthcare and technology sectors, and the head offices of major national associations. What sets us apart: \u2022 The University of Ottawa ranks among the top 10 research universities in Canada and the top 200 universities in the world. \u2022 We have been named one of Canada\u2019s Top 100 Employers for more than a decade. \u2022 The University boasts over 25 research centres and institutes and 26 institutional core facilities, hosts networks of centres of excellence and is affiliated with six hospital research institutes. Its world-class research facilities will soon include the Advanced Medical Research Centre, which will bring together researchers from many disciplines, clinicians, and start-up companies under one roof to accelerate the development of new treatments and therapies for pressing health concerns. \u2022 One of uOttawa\u2019s greatest strengths is the diversity of its people, who work, live and play in an environment that values meaningful connections, acceptance, and a sense of belonging. \u2022 The University is located in Ottawa, one of the country\u2019s most beautiful, safest and cleanest cities. Consistently ranked among the most liveable cities in the world, Ottawa offers culturally vibrant urban living minutes away from the great outdoors. It is home to the most educated workforce in Canada, attracting a host of international firms. Find out more about the University of Ottawa . The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Replacement-Professor---Sociology-and-Anthropology_JR38076-1": {"title": "Replacement Professor - Sociology and Anthropology", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-24\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: Temporary replacement of a regular position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: School of Sociological and Anthropological Studies_FT Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/07/24 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): The School of Sociological and Anthropological Studies is accepting applications for one (1) position of Replacement Professor.\u00a0 We are looking for candidates with expertise in the following areas: methodology and research design at the undergraduate and graduate levels; qualitative and/or quantitative research methodology practicums; work with civil society organizations, particularly in francophone contexts. Expertise in experiential learning is considered an asset. Priority will be given to candidates who are able to teach in both French and English from the outset. The University of Ottawa is recognized nationally and internationally as a leading university and the largest bilingual university (French and English) in the world. Located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe-Algonquin people, in the heart of downtown Ottawa, it contributes significantly to the economy, culture and quality of life of Canada\u2019s capital. TITLE OF THE POSITION: Replacement Professor DUTIES Teaching six courses per academic year at the undergraduate and graduate levels; Participating in academic and administrative activities Other activities as specified in the collective agreement QUALIFICATIONS Hold a Ph.D. in Sociology. Candidates who are close to completing a Ph.D. may be considered; Demonstrate excellence or the potential for excellence in teaching and research BILINGUALISM The University of Ottawa is a bilingual institution. Active bilingualism (English-French) is an advantage at the time of hiring. TERMS AND SALARY The initial appointment will begin on January 1, 2027, for two (2) years and will be renewable for a further period of up to three (3) years, to end on June 30, 2031. The salary scale for replacement professors is dependent on academic rank and starts at $99,377 (as of May 1, 2025). STARTING DATE: January 1, 2027 BENEFITS PACKAGE The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability leave, basic group life insurance, supplementary health insurance, a pension plan and optional life insurance. LOCATION OF WORK School of Sociological and Anthropological Studies, University of Ottawa, 120 University, Ottawa, ON, K1N 6N5. The successful candidate must reside in or be willing to relocate to the Ottawa-Gatineau region. CONTACT INFORMATION Director, School of Sociological and Anthropological Studies, Faculty of Social Sciences, Ottawa, ON K1N 6N5.\u00a0 Email socantdr@uottawa.ca APPLICATION DEADLINE The deadline for applications is\u00a0 August 28, 2026 ; however, applications may be considered after this date until the position is filled. Applications must be submitted electronically via the University of Ottawa Academic Careers website. A complete application must include the following elements: Curriculum vitae; A letter describing their teaching and research experience and interests; A clear statement of their level of fluency in French and English. Applicants must be prepared to have three referees submit letters of reference upon request. The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Professor-in-Accounting-at-the-rank-of-Assistant-or-Associate_JR37760": {"title": "Professor in Accounting at the rank of Assistant or Associate", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-15\nFull time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Job Family: Academic Leadership and Faculty Union affiliation: APUO Faculty/Department: Telfer School of Management_FT Campus: Main Campus Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/07/15 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): Help build a better Canada through better business. Join the Telfer School of Management at the University of Ottawa, a dynamic community united in the spirit of collegiality and driven by the vision of a Greener, Healthier, Happier and Wealthier, more prosperous future for all. The Telfer School of Management invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in accounting at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor commencing on July 1, 2027. Title of Position: Professor in accounting at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. The rank is commensurate with experience. Duties: Academic staff functions include, in varying proportions: teaching, research, and academic service activities. Scholarly and research activities: \u00a0develop and maintain an active research agenda in the field of accounting and related areas such as auditing. Teaching activities: teaching, as assigned, at the undergraduate and graduate levels; supervise in graduate programs, as required. Academic service activities: The candidate will be an active member of the Accounting and Finance section. Administrative duties will be agreed upon with the section and the Dean. Preferences: Preference will be given to candidates with strong ability to teach in both official languages (French and English) and who hold a Canadian CPA designation. Terms: New tenure-track position . Wage: The salary scale for an assistant professor starts at $99,377 (as of May 1, 2025). Benefits package: The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package , which includes long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses. Location of work:\u00a0 Telfer School of Management, University of Ottawa, Desmarais Hall, Ottawa ON K1N 6N5. Contact information: Telfer Recruitment Team, Telfer School of Management, Ottawa ON K1N 6N5. recruit@telfer.uottawa.ca . Skills requirements: Education: Ph.D. in accounting or in a related discipline. Candidates close to completion may also be considered. Experience: Commensurate with experience, demonstrate a strong record of, or clear potential for, strong scholarly research as evidenced by publications, competitive funding, and a well-articulated research program. Demonstrated, or strong potential for, teaching excellence. Demonstrated ability to teach Management Accounting in French at both undergraduate and graduate levels. Demonstrate commitment to, or strong potential for, service, including committee work, curriculum development, or student-centered activities, among others. Language: Excellent communication skills in\u00a0French. Passive knowledge of one\u2019s second official language (English) is a requirement for tenure. English-French bilingualism is an asset. Application Deadline : Applications received prior to August 24, 2026 , will be given first consideration. Applications received after that date will be considered until the position is filled. Applications must contain: 1) cover letter, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) research statement 4) statement of teaching philosophy, 5) recent teaching evaluation reports, and 6) the names and contact information of three individuals who may be contacted for references. More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Apply online through the University of Ottawa Careers portal. General inquiries and reference letters may be sent to recruit@telfer.uottawa.ca . About Telfer The Telfer School of Management is located on the unceded territory of the Anishinaabe Algonquin people in the heart of Ottawa, the capital of Canada, a G7 nation. Offering a bilingual environment, our school is the proud academic home to some 5,000 students, 100 full-time faculty members, and more than 35,000 alumni. We are driven by a passion for putting sustainability and social responsibility, healthcare innovation, workplace well-being and entrepreneurship at the centre of business decision-making. At Telfer, everything we do \u2014 from teaching to research to policy advice \u2014 is firmly aligned with helping Canada reach its potential. We believe that better business practice means a better Canada and, ultimately, a better world. Why choose Telfer: \u2022 Telfer is one of only two Canadian institutions accredited by all three international sanctioning bodies for business and management schools (AACSB, EQUIS and AMBA). \u2022 Newly hired professors receive generous research funding and support for research grant applications. \u2022 We are part of an ecosystem that includes six affiliated hospital research institutes, Canada\u2019s largest technology park in Kanata North, and government and business organizations, offering countless opportunities for research and academic partnerships. Our school is located within a short walk from Parliament Hill. \u2022 A testament to its teaching and research excellence, Telfer is currently the home of a Tier 1 Canada Research Chair in\u00a0Social and Inclusive Entrepreneurship and a Tier 2 Canada Research Chair in\u00a0Data Analytics for Health Systems Transformation, in addition to approximately 30 named chairs, professorships and fellowships. \u2022 We promote an environment where professors have the time and freedom to pursue transformative research and teaching. We highly value academic freedom and believe strongly that a diversity of viewpoints and vigorous debate are essential to scholarly integrity and success. Our school champions an equitable, diverse and inclusive culture in its academic programs and among its faculty, students and staff. Find out more about the Telfer School of Management . About uOttawa Founded in 1848, the University of Ottawa is the largest bilingual (French-English) university in the world, with 10 faculties and more than 50,000 students registered in undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral studies. Renowned for its expertise in public policy, health sciences, and law and social justice, uOttawa benefits from its close proximity to the seat of the federal government and to diplomatic missions, vibrant and innovative healthcare and technology sectors, and the head offices of major national associations. What sets us apart: \u2022 The University of Ottawa ranks among the top 10 research universities in Canada and the top 200 universities in the world. \u2022 We have been named one of Canada\u2019s Top 100 Employers for more than a decade. \u2022 The University boasts over 25 research centres and institutes and 26 institutional core facilities, hosts networks of centres of excellence and is affiliated with six hospital research institutes. Its world-class research facilities will soon include the Advanced Medical Research Centre, which will bring together researchers from many disciplines, clinicians, and start-up companies under one roof to accelerate the development of new treatments and therapies for pressing health concerns. \u2022 One of uOttawa\u2019s greatest strengths is the diversity of its people, who work, live and play in an environment that values meaningful connections, acceptance, and a sense of belonging. \u2022 The University is located in Ottawa, one of the country\u2019s most beautiful, safest and cleanest cities. Consistently ranked among the most liveable cities in the world, Ottawa offers culturally vibrant urban living minutes away from the great outdoors. It is home to the most educated workforce in Canada, attracting a host of international firms. Find out more about the University of Ottawa . The University of Ottawa is committed to ensuring equity, diversity, and inclusion in the scholarly and leadership environments of our students, staff, and faculty. Accordingly, we strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples in Canada, visible minorities members (racialized persons), persons with disabilities, women, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with equitable, diverse, and inclusive communities. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups are asked to complete the confidential Self-Identification Questionnaire, to be completed at the time of application. \u00a0 All qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by contacting the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations by email at vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides training to staff members and to their spouses in their second official language. \u00a0 Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Professor-in-Accounting--open-rank-_JR37755-1": {"title": "Professor in Accounting (open rank)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-15\