e du vocabulaire : origines, formation et rapports des mots entre eux. Analyse de r\u00e8gles d'emploi \u00e0 l'oral et \u00e0 l'\u00e9crit. Analyse et cr\u00e9ation de champs s\u00e9mantiques et lexicaux gr\u00e2ce \u00e0 des t\u00e2ches exploratoires. Activit\u00e9s \u00e0 partir d'\u00e9chantillons langagiers authentiques. Exploitation de ressources lexicales. Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/01 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/01 Expected Enrolment: 30 Approval date: 2026/10/01 Number of credits: 3 Work Hours: 39 Hourly Rate: Cours de langue / Language Course: $203.54 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: D Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Fran\u00e7ais | French Competence in second language: N/A Course Schedule: Lundi | Monday 17:30-19:00 Mercredi | Wednesday 17:30-19:00 - Cours en pr\u00e9sentiel Requirements: Maitrise en didactique du fran\u00e7ais langue seconde ou dans un domaine pertinent \u00e0 l\u2019enseignement du fran\u00e7ais langue seconde. Deux ans d\u2019exp\u00e9rience de l\u2019enseignement du FLS au niveau universitaire, ou \u00e9quivalent. Familiarit\u00e9 avec des logiciels et la technologie pertinente pour l\u2019enseignement des cours de fran\u00e7ais langue seconde. Connaissance de la p\u00e9dagogie du vocabulaire en fran\u00e7ais langue seconde d\u00e9montr\u00e9e par le CV. Tout professeur ayant enseign\u00e9 un cours FLS/ESL \u00e0 l'ILOB avant le 1er janvier 2018 est consid\u00e9r\u00e9 comme r\u00e9pondant \u00e0 l'exigence de dipl\u00f4me. Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/XMLNAME--APTPUO---BMG5111-B---Winter-2027_JR39620": {"title": "\u00b8APTPUO - BMG5111 B - Winter 2027", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: Main Campus Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: Facult\u00e9 de g\u00e9nie / Faculty of Engineering Academic Unit: D\u00e9partement de g\u00e9nie m\u00e9canique \\\\ Department of Mechanical Engineering Course Title: Advanced Topics II Medical Informatics and Telemedicine Course Code: BMG5111 Section: B Course Description: It is vital for healthcare service provider\u2019s policy leaders, analysts, and healthcare managers to gain an understanding of how data science can be used to positively support the decision-making processes of the health services, specially in the field of medical informatics and medical diagnosis. A knowledge and skill base in data sciences is becoming an increasing desirable for individuals working in developing medical informatic applications. A strong understanding of data science enables medical engineers to forecast emerging trends, identify potential future behavior, discover patterns, inform policy, and generate meaning from the vast amount of data that streams in and through the healthcare work environments. Essential data science concepts relevant to practical applications are covered in this course including problem formulation; data acquisition; data pre-preprocessing, modeling, simulation, and analysis. Hands on experience with data science tools, techniques and technologies will be discussed. Data integration, analysis, and presentation of results; comprehensive applications data warehousing and mining, data in areas medical informatics will be explained. Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/02 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Expected Enrol