 2027 - Research Assistant -  Minahil Asim  - 95 h", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2026 Fall Semester | Trimestre d'automne Faculty: Facult\u00e9 d'\u00e9ducation / Faculty of Education Unit: ST_Minahil Asim Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Recherche / Research Course Title: n/a Course Code: n/a Section: Supervisor name (if known): Minahil Asim Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/09 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: n/a Work Start Date: novembre 15, 2026 Work End Date: janvier 31, 2027 Total Work Hours: 95 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: N/A Language of Work: Anglais | English Requirements and Nature of Work: 'The student will help me put together a grant application. It will include working on the detailed description and doing additional analysis with existing datasets to strengthen the application. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Research-Assistant_JR40584": {"title": "Research Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2026 Fall Semester | Trimestre d'automne Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des sciences sociales / Faculty of Social Sciences Unit: School of International Development and Global Studies_Christine Gibb_JM Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Recherche / Research Course Title: N/A Course Code: N/A Section: Supervisor name (if known): Christine Gibb & James Delaney Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/06 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: N/A Work Start Date: novembre 09, 2026 Work End Date: d\u00e9cembre 22, 2026 Total Work Hours: 42 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: N/A Language of Work: Anglais | English, Fran\u00e7ais | French Requirements and Nature of Work: Responsibilities: Plan and coordinate logistics for the 19 November 2026 and 21 January 2027 workshops, including booking rooms, catering, and audio-visual and IT support\u00a0 Manage participant registration and communications with graduate students at the four partner universities\u00a0 Set up and test AI-mediated translation so participants can follow sessions in English or French\u00a0 Update the GDPP Hub website with event information and project outputs\u00a0 Liaise with graduate student association volunteers and coordinate their roles on event days Provide on-site support and take notes during the workshops Help the team keep track of tasks, timelines and event budgets. Qualifications: Required Enrolled in an undergraduate program at the University of Ottawa\u00a0\u00a0 Experience coordinating events or projects, whether in an academic, community or professional setting\u00a0 Strong organizational skills and attention to detail, with the ability to juggle several deadlines\u00a0 Strong written and spoken communication skills in English and French\u00a0 Comfortable with Microsoft 365 tools (Outlook, Teams, Forms, Excel) Be available all day, in person for both workshops \u00a0 Assets Experience updating websites\u00a0\u00a0 Experience with AI translation, interpretation or captioning tools\u00a0 Studies and/or interest in international development, knowledge mobilization or research communications. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Research-Assistant_JR40583": {"title": "Research Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2026 Fall Semester | Trimestre d'automne Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des sciences sociales / Faculty of Social Sciences Unit: School of International Development and Global Studies_Christine Gibb_JM Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Recherche / Research Course Title: N/A Course Code: N/A Section: Supervisor name (if known): Christine Gibb Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/26 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/06 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: N/A Work Start Date: novembre 09, 2026 Work End Date: d\u00e9cembre 22, 2026 Total Work Hours: 24 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: N/A Language of Work: Anglais | English, Fran\u00e7ais | French Requirements and Nature of Work: Responsibilities: Work with workshop presenters as they develop and prepare their sessions\u00a0 Set up and maintain a mentorship database, match participants with mentors, and coordinate mentorship activities from December 2026 to April 2027\u00a0 Take notes during workshop sessions\u00a0 Give feedback to participating graduate students on their RDM and KM plans, policy briefs and other knowledge products\u00a0 Compile the written and video proceedings of both workshops for publication, in French and English, on the GDPP Hub website\u00a0 Support the research team with other project tasks as needed Qualifications: Required \u00a0 Enrolled in a doctoral program at the University of Ottawa, ideally in international development or a related social science discipline\u00a0 Familiarity with knowledge mobilization and research data management, and a real interest in getting research to non-academic audiences\u00a0 Strong writing and editing skills, and the ability to give clear, constructive feedback to peers\u00a0 Well organized and reliable, able to keep track of many moving parts and deadlines\u00a0 Ability to work in English and French: strong proficiency in one and working knowledge of the other\u00a0 Be available all day, in person for both workshops Assets \u00a0 Experience producing policy briefs, op-eds, podcasts or other knowledge products\u00a0 Experience coordinating mentorship or peer-support programs\u00a0 Research or professional experience in global development, social justice or forced displacement\u00a0 Familiarity with data management planning tools such as the DMP Assistant. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/j