ng Type: Cours pr\u00e9cis / Specific course Course Title: Computer Systems Design Course Code: CEG3156 Section: A Supervisor name (if known): R. Abielmona Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/05 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 2 Expected Enrolment: N/A Work Start Date: January 01, 2027 Work End Date: April 30, 2027 Total Work Hours: 130 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: in person Language of Work: Anglais | English Requirements and Nature of Work: Nature of Work: Correct assignments/quizzes, prepare and give tutorials, lab work and consultation hours. Setting up and testing experiments in computer architecture. Proctoring. Minimum Requirements: Preference will be given to candidates who have completed this course or its equivalent and its prerequisite(s) with a mark of A or better. \u00a0Attend a TA workshop if necessary. \u00a0Must be ready for an interview if considered for this position. \u00a0If this course contains DGD and/or TUT and/or LAB sessions, the candidate must be available for these. Excellent knowledge of Altera, Quartus II, Modelsim, VHDL, ASM, RTL, RISC processors. Please note that the teaching method will be delivered: in person. \u200bNOTE: le nombre de postes et le nombre d'heures offertes sont tous deux assujettis aux inscriptions finales dans ce cours. Please note that the number of hours and positions offered may vary due to enrolments in that course. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor---Indigenous-Education_JR40299-1": {"title": "Assistant Professor - Indigenous Education", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nJob Type: Academic Regular Faculty/Department: Formation \u00e0 l'enseignement_FT This position is a designated hire restricted to First Nations, Metis, and Inuit (FNMI) candidates. While all qualified individuals are encouraged to apply, priority in selection will be given to candidates who identify as First Nations, M\u00e9tis, or Inuit. Applicants are invited to identify as First Nations, Metis, and/or Inuit in their cover letter by highlighting their lived experiences, connections to community, and research praxis. Shortlisted candidates will be required to submit documentation according to the university's Policy 135 for FNMI membership/citizenship verification for all appointments, awards, and funding opportunities for faculty, staff, graduate students and post-doctoral fellows prior to interviewing. INDIGENOUS EDUCATION \u2013 Assistant Professor (English Program) \u00a0 As part of our commitment to reconciliation with Indigenous peoples, our dedication to Indigenous Research Excellence and the expansion of our research and teacher education programs, the Faculty of Education at the University of Ottawa invites applications for one tenure track position in Indigenous Education. \u00a0 The Faculty of Education at the University of Ottawa is located on the ancestral territories of the Algonquin people, in the heart of the nation\u2019s capital. With more than fifty professors, it offers an exceptional intellectual environment for its students, researchers and professors. Open to the world, the Faculty is committed to providing students with a rich and stimulating experience and to promoting excellence in research. This recruitment reflects the current Faculty of Education\u2019s strategic plan, which emphasizes social and ecological justice, well-being, and excellence and innovation in teaching and research. This position is a designated hire restricted to First Nations, Metis, and Inuit (FNMI) candidates. While all qualified individuals are encouraged to apply, priority in selection will be given to candidates who identify as First Nations, M\u00e9tis, or Inuit. Applicants are invited to identify as First Nations, Metis, and/or Inuit in their cover letter by highlighting their lived experiences, connections to community, and research praxis. Shortlisted candidates will be required to submit documentation according to the university's Policy 135 for FNMI membership/citizenship verification for all appointments, awards, and funding opportunities for faculty, staff, graduate students and post-doctoral fellows prior to interviewing. We are seeking an Indigenous researcher with expertise in Indigenous education and experience collaborating with Indigenous communities in Canada. This individual will also provide leadership in rebuilding, redesigning, and relaunching our Indigenous Teacher Education Program, in partnership with communities and under faculty direction. \u00a0 Overall duties: The overall duties of the position include, in varying proportions and in accordance with the Collective Agreement in effect between the University of Ottawa and The Association of Professors of the University of Ottawa (APUO): a) teaching activities; b) scholarly activities in the form of research, artistic or literacy creation, or professional work; c) academic service activities; d) supervision of graduate students. This individual will also serve as program director of the Indigenous Teacher Education Program (ITEP) and lead the process of rebuilding, redesigning and relaunching ITEP through community partnership. The successful candidate will primarily teach in the Faculty\u2019s English-language programs. \u00a0 Terms: New tenure-track position (Assistant Professor), starting on July 1st, 2027. \u00a0 Wage: Salary scale for an Assistant Professor starts at $99,377 (as of May 1, 2025). \u00a0 Benefits package: The University of Ottawa provides a complete benefits package which include long-term disability insurance, basic group life insurance, health insurance, University of Ottawa pension plan and optional life insurance, as well as reimbursement of eligible relocation expenses. \u00a0 Skills requirements: Education: Ph.D. in Education or equivalent in a field related to the position. Work Experience: A demonstrated excellent track record of research in Indigenous Education in collaboration with Indigenous communities in Canada. A demonstrated ability to teach at the undergraduate and/or graduate levels in a context of interdisciplinary collaboration. Ability to teach hybrid and on-line courses. Passive knowledge of the other official language in Canada is a requirement for tenure. An Indigenous language is considered an asset to this position. Your application should include (i) your curriculum vitae, (ii) a sample of your published work, (iii) the names and contact information for three referees, (iv) a transcript for each university degree awarded, and (v) a cover letter describing your interest in this position, your program of research and teaching interests. We encourage candidates to highlight their Indigenous identity in their application, in alignment with the University of Ottawa\u2019s policies and commitments to reconciliation. as we understand 'Indigenous' is a broad term. One that encompasses diverse peoples with distinct identities, such as First Nations, M\u00e9tis, and Inuit. Please include a brief statement in your application to ensure the hiring committee can appropriately consider your lived experience and contributions to Indigenous education, methodologies, and community engagement. \u00a0 Location of work: University of Ottawa, Faculty of Education, Ottawa, ON K1N 6N5 \u00a0 Contact information : El Hadji Yaya Kon\u00e9, Vice-Dean, Graduate and Postdoctoral Studies, Faculty of Education, University of Ottawa, send us an email at vdprog@uottawa.ca . \u00a0 Application deadline: The consultation of applications will begin after November 22, 2026 , however we will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. \u00a0 More information regarding Academic Careers can be found online . Apply online through the University of Ottawa Careers portal. \u00a0 \u00a0 In accordance with government policy, all qualified candidates are invited to apply; however, preference will be given to Canadians and permanent residents. When submitting your application, please indicate if you are legally entitled to work in Canada. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa provides accommodations for applicants with disabilities throughout the recruitment process. If you are invited to proceed in the selection process, please notify us of any accommodations that you require by emailing the Office of the Vice-Provost, Faculty Relations, at vra.affairesprofessorales@uOttawa.ca . Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. \u00a0 The University of Ottawa is proud of its more than 160-year tradition of bilingualism. Through its Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute, the University provides second-language training to staff members and their spouses."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/APTPUO---Winter-2027---ESL1113--Section-A00_JR40451": {"title": "APTPUO - Winter 2027 - ESL1113, Section A00", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: Main Campus Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des arts / Faculty of Arts Academic Unit: Institut des langues officielles et du bilinguisme \\\\ Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute (OLBI) Course Title: What\u2019s the Rule? Grammar at the Beginner Level Course Code: ESL1113 Section: A00 Course Description: Introduction to and practice with the fundamental elements of the English verb system, question formation, adjectives, and pronouns. Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/23 Expected Enrolment: 30 Approval date: 2026/10/22 Number of credits: 3 Work Hours: 39 Hourly Rate: Cours de langue / Language Course: $203.54 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: D Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Anglais | English Competence in second language: N/A Course Schedule: Mardi | Tuesday 13:00-14:30 Jeudi | Thursday 11:30-13:00 - This course will be in person. Requirements: Master\u2019s degree in teaching English as a second language, or a discipline relevant to English as a second language teaching. Two years of experience teaching ESL at the university level, or equivalent. Familiarity with software and technologies relevant to teach English as a second language. A professor who has taught an FLS / ESL course at OLBI before January 1, 2018 is considered to meet the diploma requirement. Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Agente-principale--agent-principal--Traitement-de-dons_JR40250": {"title": "Senior Officer, Gift Processing", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): N/A Job Family: Advancement and Alumni Relations # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Gift Processing Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 08 Salary Range: $68\u00a0149,00 - $86\u00a0083,00 University Advancement plays a pivotal role in fostering meaningful connections between the University and its alumni, donors, and partners. Our team is dedicated to advancing the University's mission by securing philanthropic support, engaging with our vibrant alumni community, and building partnerships that drive innovation and excellence. Through strategic initiatives and collaborative efforts, we help shape the future of the University, ensuring that it continues to thrive as a leading institution of higher education and research. Together, we empower our students, faculty and researchers to make a lasting impact on the world. ** This position is not eligible for telework ** Position purpose: Reporting to the Manager, Gift Processing, the incumbent plans and coordinates the operational activities of the Gift Processing Office, including financial transactions and internal controls, in order to provide support and advice to managers and to ensure that policies and procedures are well understood and properly implemented. Coordinates the donor acknowledgement process and contributes to database integrity and accuracy of gift details and biographical information. Checks donor accessible funds to identify irregular spending patterns.\u00a0 In this role, your responsibilities will include: Processes gifts in support of the fundraising team\u2019s philanthropic efforts. Scrutinizes all gifts and solicitation/pledge forms received to ensure that information is valid and accurate. Enters data on complex gifts which require particular attention. Maintains database integrity by checking for irregularities among gift payments and among the biographical information submitted with gifts. Monitors the accuracy of the gift information entered by other staff members, performs validation queries and implements corrective measures when inconsistencies are detected. Prepares and processes batches to efficiently process several donations at once (fundraising events, crowdfunding, third party solicitations, monthly donations through payroll deduction, pension plan deductions, direct debit by banks and major credit cards). Deposits all monies (cheques, cash or credit card payments) while following appropriate University procedures and internal controls. Produces and reconciles deposit reports (using financial system ledger) that are submitted with cash and cheques to Financial Services.\u00a0 Ensures tax receipts are issued in a timely manner based on gift receipt eligibility as indicated by the Canada Revenue Agency. What you will bring: Advanced knowledge of accounting practices normally acquired through postsecondary studies in a related discipline, or an acceptable equivalent combination of education and work experience Minimum 3 years of demonstrated experience in a similar role Spoken and written proficiency in both official languages (French and English) Strong organizational skills and ability to set priorities Ability to take responsibility and to work independently Excellent customer service and good interpersonal skills\u00a0 Ability to work well as a team member Knowledge of philanthropic databases (Raiser\u2019s Edge) considered an asset Knowledge of Canada Revenue Agency rules and regulations on charities Office experience in using word processing, spreadsheets and databases Very close attention to detail to ensure highly accurate data entry and analysis Ability to understand and follow directions and explain complex policies and procedures Good judgment and ability to use sensitive and confidential information appropriately Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Manager--Animal-Care-Operations_JR40352": {"title": "Assistant Manager, Animal Care Operations", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): N/A Job Family: Laboratory Support # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Animal Care and Veterinary Service Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 10 Salary Range: $85,040.00 - $107,421.00 Position Purpose Reporting to the Manager, Animal Care Operations, the incumbent allocates and manages the human resource assignments for animal care and use activities at the uOttawa animal facilities. Contributes to (in-vivo) research viability and success by liaising with members of ACVS staff, researchers, students and members of other departments or institutes as required to assure training and skill sets of ACVS staff meet institutional needs. Assists the Manager, Animal Care Operations to ensure that animal facility equipment and overall facility operations are adequate to assure institutional compliance with the regulatory requirements and to promote excellence in the conduct of animal based academic and experimental research protocol execution. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Develop, implement and supervise equitable daily tasks assignments in all areas of the Roger Guindon Hall and Vanier Hall animal facilities to ensure adequate staffing coverage and the smooth running of operations. \u00a0This includes but is not limited to coordination of cage wash activities, \u00a0work schedules for species specific husbandry care, veterinary technical nursing and/or experimental treatments to resident animals to ensure appropriate research protocol and housing requirements are achieved, documented and reported. This includes weekends, statutory holidays and other circumstances such as illness or accident. Communicate with research teams on various upcoming research projects to schedule support staff accordingly. \u00a0The incumbent must be able to appropriately and continuously assess human resource needs and equipment needs to ensure adequate staffing levels and required equipment at all times. \u00a0 Coordinate the daily operations of the RGN and VNR vivaria, ensuring compliance with the regulatory requirements of the Canadian Council on Animal Care as stated in the Guide to the Care and Use of Experimental Animals, volume 2, 1993 (the Guide), the Animals for Research Act, R.S.O. 1990, c. A-27 (the Act) and the University of Ottawa Policy 31 (the Policy). Schedule, assign and implement the sanitation and biosecurity/quarantine protocols within the facilities to preserve and protect animal health and reduce research variables. Communicate, consult and liaise with team leaders, and the manager of operations daily to ensure excellent team communication and smooth running of operations. \u00a0Communication with the \u00a0veterinarian, researcher and/or research personnel will also be required to ensure pertinent information is obtained and/or disseminated to appropriate parties in regards to animal health, research projects, equipment inventory and/or facility function. Assists the Manager, Animal Care Operations in the continuing assessment of operational efficiency, development, implementation and review of Standard Operating Procedures (SOP) to ensure the highest standards in animal care, optimal operational efficiency and compliance with all regulatory bodies. Identify and communicate directly and promptly to the Manager, Animal Care Operations any issues that may negatively impact animal care and welfare or result in non-compliance with accepted guidelines and standards.\u00a0 What you will bring: Sound theoretical and practical knowledge of applied veterinary technical and surgical nursing normally acquired through a formal post-secondary technical training program and 5 years\u2019 experience managing or supervising activities in an animal research environment. Training can be acquired through a diploma from a CVMA recognized Veterinary Technical program or a Bachelor of Science degree from a relevant discipline and ideally the incumbent will hold an RLAT. certification with the Canadian Association of Laboratory Animal Science (CALAS). Five (5) years of experience managing or supervising activities in an animal research environment. Experience in human resource policy administration and team/ conflict management. Demonstrate an ability to gain consensus and achieve collaborations. Experienced with the development of complex work schedules. Comprehensive knowledge and experience in the development and implementation of standard operational procedures for an animal research facility, including bio-exclusion, bio- containment methods. Commitment to client service through excellence in interpersonal and communication skills and service delivery. Commitment to ensuring a safe working environment through ongoing inspections, staff training and review of safety standards. Experience using computer systems and various software such as Windows, word processors, spreadsheets, e-calendars, databases, email and Internet. Ability and willingness to work after hours, weekends and statutory holidays as assigned. Ability and willingness to be on-call on a rotational basis as part of standard schedule. Excellent communications skills in both official languages. Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Coordonnatrice-principale--coordonnateur-principal--Dveloppement_JR40298": {"title": "Senior Coordinator, Advancement", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Temporary replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 12 Job Family: Advancement and Alumni Relations # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Office of Advancement - Philanthropy - Planned Giving Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 08 Salary Range: $68\u00a0149,00 - $86\u00a0083,00 University Advancement plays a pivotal role in fostering meaningful connections between the University and its alumni, donors, and partners. Our team is dedicated to advancing the University's mission by securing philanthropic support, engaging with our vibrant alumni community, and building partnerships that drive innovation and excellence. Through strategic initiatives and collaborative efforts, we help shape the future of the University, ensuring that it continues to thrive as a leading institution of higher education and research. Together, we empower our students, faculty and researchers to make a lasting impact on the world. Position Purpose This position plays a critical role in contributing to the University of Ottawa\u2019s fundraising success by providing administrative, operational, and project management support to the advancement team. This position ensures the efficient execution of major, principal, planned, and annual gift fundraising strategies and initiatives. The incumbent manages the prospect pipeline in collaboration with fundraisers, cultivates strong donor relationships through stewardship activities, and facilitates seamless communication and coordination among internal and external stakeholders, including faculties and donors. Additionally, the role involves organizing prospect and donor meetings, preparing fundraising communication materials, and maintaining meticulous donor records. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Fundraising Operations & Project Coordination: Provides administrative and operational support to ensure the smooth execution of fundraising strategies across major, principal, planned, and annual giving portfolios. Assists with the planning and coordination of projects, events, and initiatives that drive donor engagement and revenue growth. Supports fundraisers by managing timelines, logistics, and follow-up activities. Prospect Pipeline & Records Management: Manages and monitors the donor and prospect pipeline in close collaboration with fundraisers to support effective cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship strategies. Maintains accurate and current records in the CRM and internal systems, tracks engagement activities, and contributes to proposal development and prospect assignments. Ensures data integrity, generates fundraising reports, and gathers key insights to enhance prospect profiles, enabling informed decision-making, accurate forecasting, and targeted fundraising efforts. Donor & Stakeholder Engagement: Cultivates and maintains strong relationships with donors, faculties, and internal stakeholders. Organizes prospect and donor meetings, prepares detailed briefing notes, and ensures timely, personalized follow-up. Coordinates strategic donor engagement and stewardship activities that reinforce trust, demonstrate impact, and align with University priorities. Gift Implementation & Compliance: Collaborates with academic and development leadership to align fund usage with institutional priorities and long-term goals. Ensures strategic management of scholarships and fund allocations to maximize student impact and reporting accuracy.\u00a0 Communications & Proposal Development: Facilitates the creation and delivery of high-quality fundraising communications and donor materials, including pitch decks, proposals, and reports. Ensures messaging is consistent, professional, and tailored to donor interests. Coordinates the translation, review, and approval of communications, and supports faculty and fundraiser collaborations to strengthen donor outreach. Other related duties: Performs other duties in support of the unit coordination efforts as assigned. What you will bring An undergraduate degree, preferably in administration, business, arts, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. A minimum of three (3) years of relevant experience in a field related to the responsibilities of the position.\u00a0 Bilingualism \u2013 French and English (spoken and written). Proven experience in administration and project management.\u00a0 Exceptional planning and time management skills.\u00a0 Superior written and communication skills in English and French.\u00a0 Strong analytical skills with the ability to synthesize information from various sources and produce clear, accurate reports.\u00a0 Ability to deal with the public, senior officials and high-profile stakeholders.\u00a0 Sound judgement, with excellent listening, problem solving, priority setting, and organizational skills. Proficient in databases, spreadsheets word processing, e-mail and Internet tools, with the ability to analyze and manage data via a computerized system.\u00a0 Ability to demonstrate a high level of professionalism and able to exercise professional discretion.\u00a0 Proven ability to take initiative and work in collaboration with volunteers.\u00a0 Experience or knowledge of post-secondary environment. *This position may be subject to a telework agreement. #LI-HYBRID Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Director--Undergraduate-Medical-Education_JR40377": {"title": "Director, Undergraduate Medical Education", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): N/A Job Family: Academic Program Management # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Office of the Chief Operation Officer Campus: Roger Guindon Hall Union Affiliation: N/A Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: Non-Union Grade NM3 Salary Range: $132,504.00 - $165,630.00 About the Faculty of Medicine Welcome to the University of Ottawa Faculty of Medicine, the first and largest bilingual medical faculty in Canada. Our learners have access to state-of-the art facilities and specialized institutions as they learn alongside world-class physicians and scientists. Consistently ranked among the top 10 medical schools in Canada, our faculty is among the top faculties of medicine in the world for research impact in biomedical and health sciences. Position Purpose: Provides strategic and operational direction across the Undergraduate Medical Education (UGME) portfolio. Ensures effective governance, leaner centered service delivery and compliance with accreditation and regulatory requirements, and alignment of human, financial, technological and physical resources with the Faculty\u2019s academic mission . Aligns the office\u2019s human, financial, and material resources with the program and Faculty objectives. Provides advice and guidance to Faculty Leadership, Hospital Leadership,\u00a0 Vice-Deans, Assistant Deans and Academic Directors and Leaders on issues related to Undergraduate Medical Education. Supports institutional decision-making by providing strategic advice, risk analysis, operational planning, and evidence-informed recommendations to Faculty and UGME leadership. Specific Accountabilities: Strategic Priorities and Operations: Plans and directs operations to achieve UGME\u2019s strategic goals, working within financial and Human Resources (HR) parameters. Develops short and long-term goals to achieve the UGME vision, implements initiatives supporting the effective delivery of the UGME program, including process improvements and development of IT tools and resources. Directs operations team , and works in coordination with UGME academic leaders, hospital liaisons and other internal and external interested parties to deliver the program UGME. Leads complex operational change initiatives including organizational restructuring, digital transformation, policy implementation and new operating models. Proactively identifies persistent operational and staffing issues and brings options and recommendations to the Vice-Dean. Leads operational planning and implementation for MD curriculum renewal, including scheduling, assessment and data systems. Academic Program Management: Directs the successful outcome of operations of the MD Program, delivered in 2 language streams for 750 medical students, 1,000 visiting medical students, 2,000 teaching faculty members, 145 Academic Directors and Leaders. Areas of responsibilities are comprised of Admissions, Curriculum Design and Implementation, Electives, Distributed Medical Education, Indigenous Program, Student Affairs Office, Office of Accreditation and Continuous Quality Improvement and UGME Finance. Ensures students\u2019 compliance with mandated/legal requirements for clinical placements in affiliated partner teaching hospitals and clinical environments in collaboration with the Clinical Placement Risk Management Office. Collaborates with the Faculty of Medicine\u2019s International Global Health Office in placement of visiting students from international partners. Leadership and Human Resources: Provides leadership and oversight for administrative managers and supervisors within the team. Directs all human resources functions for team members, including recruitment, onboarding, training and development, performance management, resource planning, goal setting, and employee relations. Establish effective operational structures that promote coordination, efficiency, and service excellence, while fostering a culture of continuous improvement and responsiveness to internal and external parties needs. Ensures the team has the training, resources, and support required to perform effectively, and champion professional growth through mentoring, cross-training, workshops, and other development opportunities. Develop workforce plans that address capacity, succession, workload distribution, role clarity, talent development, resilience and continuity of UGME operations. Communication and Relationship Building: Ensures effective communication with key interested parties in a timely and accurate manner to ensure their compliance with UGME, Faculty of Medicine, university, as well as provincial and federal governing bodies\u2019 rules and regulations. Build and maintain operational relationships with teaching hospitals, distributed medical education sites, regional partners, clinical departments, preceptors, community organizations, provincial bodies and national medical education organizations. Ensure partnership processes support learner placement, clinical teaching, reporting, risk management, funding, service delivery and compliance obligations. \u00a0Oversees production and distribution of annual reports to each of the 16 Basic Science and Clinical Departments. Finance Management: In collaboration with the Vice-Dean, the Director is responsible for all aspects of financial management within UGME including the preparation and management of 111 operating budgets and teaching remuneration budgets in excess of $11 million, conducting complex analysis (11 sub budgets) and variance reporting on a quarterly basis. Responsible for the remuneration of teaching activities of over 2,600 faculty members to the upwards of 20,000 teaching activities per year which represent a budget of 4M of the overall budget. Provides recommendations on financial matters to the Vice-Dean, Assistant Deans and Directors, and financial managers in the teaching hospitals. Works with and reports financial activity through to the Director, Financial Services at the Faculty of Medicine. Effectively applies accounting practices of the University of Ottawa to ensure compliance with financial controls. Manages funds received from both the Ministry of Health as well as the Ministry of Colleges and Universities, in addition to funds received from the University of Ottawa, as well as individual bursaries.\u00a0 Manages distribution of funds distributed to students, hospitals, faculty, and allied health care partners at various times throughout the year. Accreditation Support: In collaboration with the Vice Dean, leads the administration of the accreditation process of CACMS to ensure ongoing compliance to required accreditation standards of the MD Program. Responsible for achieving institutional standards in the areas of institutional governance; learners, teachers, and administrative personnel; for the purpose of continuous improvement. Identifies and directs the implementation of improvements for areas identified as weaknesses to ensure positive accreditation status. Governance and Committee Support: Lead and coordinate the administrative infrastructure supporting UGME governance, including committees related to curriculum, assessment, promotions, admissions, student affairs, accreditation, professionalism, and continuous quality improvement. Ensure committee processes, records, decisions, follow-up actions, reporting cycles and escalation pathways are well coordinated, timely, accurate and aligned with Faculty, University and accreditation requirements. Project and Process Management: Identifies and oversees projects to identify business process improvements using Lean Six Sigma methodology to increase efficiency and achieve continuous quality improvement. Reviews requirements for new policies or updates to policies and procedures and develops these for the purpose of implementation to ensure that the UGME program is meeting accreditation standards as set out by CACMS as well as university regulations. Recommends, plans, and oversees the implementation of information technology projects to increase self-serve opportunities, increase efficiency of reporting cycles and increase the quality of output. \u00a0 Knowledge, Experience and Skills: Essential Qualifications: \u00a0 Knowledge of management administration normally acquired through a university degree or through equivalent education and experience Minimum of 12 years in related roles, including 5 years of management responsibilities in all aspects of human resources management, budget management, project planning and operations management. \u00a0 Other Qualifications: Strong problem solving and conflict resolution skills Knowledge and experience in financial management including forecasting, reporting, and analysis. Ability to work autonomously with little to no direction, ability to conduct research to make effective decisions based on facts. Ability to apply change management methodology to ensure optimal project success. Knowledge of the general structure of undergraduate medical education training in Canada and Ontario provincial licensing requirements is an asset Knowledge of health care system in Ontario and Canada is an asset Ability to succeed in a high-paced, team oriented, high demand environment with strict deadlines Proven negotiation skills Diplomacy and judgment Knowledge of post-secondary academic operations, international sponsorships and requirements for international residents and fellows to apply to our programs is an asset Experience in writing, interpretation and application of policies and procedures. Knowledge of accreditation processes is critical, knowledge of the CACMS is an asset Strong communications, interpersonal, team building, influencing and project management skills. Bilingualism \u2013 French and English (spoken and written). #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Intermediate-Advisor--Communications-and-Marketing_JR40314-1": {"title": "Intermediate Advisor, Communications and Marketing", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: New Position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 12 Job Family: Communications and Marketing (JF) # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Marketing and Communications Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 10 Salary Range: $85,040.00 - $107,421.00 About the Faculty of Education The Faculty of Education, along with its commitment to the promotion, advancement, and dissemination of knowledge in the field of education in general, is actively involved in teacher training. Located on the unceded ancestral lands of Algonquin peoples, the Faculty of Education is proud to promote equity, diversity, and inclusion. Moreover, according to the latest QS ranking, our faculty is ranked among the top 100 in the world, and fifth in Canada, for research. Position purpose: Reporting to the Manager of Communications and Marketing, contribute to the development and growth of programs and services offered by the sector through strategic use of marketing and communication tools. Generate web content, conceptualize and implement communications and marketing plans to project a positive image, promote the brand and increase the visibility of the sector. Act as resource person to employees of the sector on inquires related to communications and marketing to ensure compliance with established policies and standards. \u00a0 In this job, your responsibilities will include: Communications: Develop and execute an annual communications plan that supports the sector\u2019s communications needs for various internal and external target audiences. To this end, write content for annual reports, news bulletins, brochures, speeches, web, social media and press releases and coordinate translation. Perform needs and cost/benefit analyses of proposed communications plans to support the annual budget planning of the sector and ensure that related activities comply with budget limits. Marketing: Develop and implement marketing strategies for the sector contributing to the corporate marketing and communications plan. To this end, design marketing material, and collaborate with graphic designers and photography and printing resources in its production to promote the sector while ensuring compliance with graphic standards. Perform needs and cost/benefit analyses of proposed marketing plans to support the annual budget planning of the sector and ensure that related activities comply with budget limits. Quality assurance: Develop performance indicators and monitor performance by executing consultations and surveys in order to maximize the effectiveness of marketing and communications activities/products of the sector. Analyze results and coordinate improvements to optimize the use of marketing and communications tools. Trends and needs analysis: Initiate and execute market research studies and analyze results to identify potential new markets and program/service offerings. Analyze and evaluate communications and advertising needs and propose solutions contributing to annual strategic plan of the sector. Media relations: Act as the first point of contact for relations with media to facilitate responses to media requests and make referrals to subject matter experts when necessary. Client advice: Provide advice and tools to employees of the sector in the preparation of communications and promotional material to ensure visual identity and compliance to communication policies, web, accessibility and official language standards. Act as resource person for the sector for inquiries pertaining to the creation of accessible documents. Web content management: Anticipate requirements and generate web content to best meet ongoing business needs while ensuring compliance with web, official language and accessibility standards and established policies. Develop and evaluate performance indicators for web usage and usability, and coordinate improvements to maximize the effectiveness of the sector\u2019s web sites. Events management: Lead all aspects of planning and execution for small- to mid-sized events for student recruitment and other special events in order to project a positive image of the faculty and/or the university. To this end, form and chair event planning committees, identify elements and timelines, monitor and communicate progress. Develop event budgets and ensure that all activities take place within budget limitations. Develop performance indicators and monitor performance to maximize the effectiveness of liaison and recruitment activities. Liaison: Participate in internal and external meetings and events in order to facilitate outreach, engagement, and collaboration. Promote programs, developments, and ongoing activities. Represent the sector during external recruitment activities. \u00a0 What you will bring: Knowledge of marketing and communications principles normally acquired through a postsecondary degree in a related discipline or an equivalent combination of education and work experience Minimum 3 years of demonstrated experience in a similar role Advanced knowledge of computer systems and software including graphic design and web applications, social media platforms, spreadsheets, databases, Internet and e-mail Knowledge of web accessibility guidelines and experience in their application Experience in developing and implementing a communications plan Experience giving presentations to large groups in both official languages Experience in event planning and organization Experience managing projects Strong public relations, interpersonal and communication skills Strong organizational skills Ability and availability to occasionally travel outside of the city to facilitate recruitment activities, and to work outside of normal office hours Bilingualism - French and English (spoken and written) #LI-SA1 #Li-HYBRID Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Intermediate-Project-Manager_JR40353": {"title": "Intermediate Project Manager", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Temporary replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 36 Job Family: Project Management # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Assessment, Evaluation and Curriculum Office Campus: Roger Guindon Hall Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 10 Salary Range: $85,040.00 - $107,421.00 About the Faculty of Medicine Welcome to the University of Ottawa Faculty of Medicine, the first and largest bilingual medical faculty in Canada. Our learners have access to state-of-the art facilities and specialized institutions as they learn alongside world-class physicians and scientists. Consistently ranked among the top 10 medical schools in Canada, our faculty is among the top faculties of medicine in the world for research impact in biomedical and health sciences. Position purpose: Under the supervision of the person in charge of educational support, the incumbent will fully manage projects related to program review, assessment activities (including exams) and the implementation of entrustable professional activities (EPAs). The incumbent will work closely with the assistant dean, curriculum and the managers in charge of exams and evaluations. \u00a0 Specific accountabilities: Manages all aspects of projects that review study programs, activities related to pre-clerkship and clerkship exams, evaluations and entrustable professional activities (EPAs). Supports the completion of other Undergraduate Medical Education projects, including those related to certification, support to professors, and any other initiative indicated by Undergraduate Medical Education management. Creates concept documents and project charters. Establishes project schedules using gap analysis software, measures performance and, in partnership with the program director\u2019s office, takes appropriate action to complete projects. Ensures that projects run smoothly in accordance with established budgets and designs effective cost control mechanisms. In collaboration with the Assessment, Evaluation and Curriculum Office supervisor, ensures an optimal allocation of human resources to various projects. Implements and monitors the planning, quality assurance and quality control processes. Identifies, analyzes and measures risks associated with projects; designs risk mitigation strategies and contingency plans, conducts the required follow-up and documents the actions taken and results obtained. Designs and implements project communication plans. Drafts project reports and presents them to senior managers. Designs and organizes training workshops for various projects. \u00a0 Knowledge, experience and skills: Essential qualifications: Knowledge of project management principles normally acquired through postsecondary or university education Five years\u2019 experience in a position involving comparable project management responsibilities \u00a0 Other Qualifications: Professional certification in project management (PMP) is an asset Knowledge of university operations and of hospital and health-care fields Mastery of project management and planning methods, techniques and tools, including risk analysis Proven experience in designing and managing project schedules Ability to work well under pressure Advanced proficiency in using Microsoft Office software Excellent interpersonal and communication skills Ability to negotiate effectively and solve problems Fluency in English and French, both spoken and written #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/725-Parkdale---TOH-Civic/Lead-Coordinator--Event-Management_JR40283": {"title": "Lead Coordinator, Event Management", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Temporary replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 12 Job Family: Event Management # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Continuing Medical Education Campus: Roger Guindon Hall Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 09 Salary Range: $76,104.00 - $96,135.00 About the Faculty of Medicine Welcome to the University of Ottawa Faculty of Medicine, the first and largest bilingual medical faculty in Canada. Our learners have access to state-of-the art facilities and specialized institutions as they learn alongside world-class physicians and scientists. Consistently ranked among the top 10 medical schools in Canada, our faculty is among the top faculties of medicine in the world for research impact in biomedical and health sciences. Position purpose: Plans, manages and promotes events that engage alumni, raise awareness in the community, foster ties between strategic partners and the academic community, and support recruitment, fundraising and research activities. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Plans events: Plans and designs, for the entire faculty or service, various on- and off-campus events that engage alumni, raise awareness in the community, build ties between strategic partners and the academic community, and support recruitment, fundraising and research activities. To this end, discusses with clients, identifies requirements and deadlines, sets budgets, plans and coordinates all the steps required to conduct the event. Manages events: Organizes and manages all the logistics surrounding events, such as reservations, rentals, registration and invitations, catering services, equipment installation and set up of spaces. Supervises the running of these events and remains available to resolve any problems that might arise. Welcomes guests and acts as University representative and host for each event. Supervises others: Hires and trains individuals, such as volunteers and caterers, and coordinates and supervises their work to ensure that they carry out their duties as directed and to the required standard. Manages budgets: Manages budgets related to the organization and running of events, while staying within budget limits and complying with related policies, regulations and procedures. Advertising: Participates in planning and designing the marketing plans required to conduct events and maximize stakeholder awareness and participation. What you will bring: Essential Qualifications College diploma in marketing, event management, fundraising, or communications, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience Minimum five years\u2019 experience in a similar role Other Qualifications Proven ability to formulate creative ideas and to analyze, evaluate and negotiate details to create a successful event Strong interpersonal and communication skills Excellent problem-solving skills Ability to work well under pressure and meet strict deadlines Self-reliance and good judgment Project management experience Experience in supervising teams and volunteers Knowledge of budget management policies and practices Strong organizational skills Knowledge of computer systems and software such as Windows, word processors, web applications and graphic design software, social media platforms, spreadsheets, databases, presentation software, internet browsers and email Bilingualism \u2013 French and English (spoken and written) #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Lead-Visual-Technician--Marketing_JR40327-1": {"title": "Lead Visual Technician, Marketing", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: New Position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 24 Job Family: Communications and Marketing (JF) # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Content Strategic Research Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: N/A Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: Non-Union Grade NC7 - 35 hrs Salary Range: $85,910.00 - $107,388.00 Position purpose The videographer is responsible for shooting, editing and producing a wide range of video content that showcases the University's mission, values and achievements. This role involves collaborating with colleagues from the Office of the Vice-President, Research and Innovation, and with stakeholders across faculties and services, to produce video content that effectively communicates the University\u2019s brand, engages its diverse audiences and advances the uOttawa\u2019s reputation. In this role, you will: Video production: Leads the process from pre-production (storyboarding, scouting, scheduling, etc.) through to production (filming, managing logistics, capturing audio, etc.) and post-production (editing, creating motion graphics, delivering files, etc.). Creates video content that aligns with the University\u2019s goals and the communication plans developed to achieve them. Ensures that the video content produced meets high-quality standards in terms of AODA standards, visual aesthetics and storytelling effectiveness, while reflecting the University's brand.\u00a0 Creative development: Suggests ideas for video content that align with the University\u2019s communication objectives and plans. Stays abreast of industry developments and best practices while proactively applying new insights and techniques to improve efficiency and effectiveness. Continuously seeks opportunities for learning and growth by expanding their skills and knowledge in video production techniques, equipment and software. Collaboration: Works closely with the team at the Office of the Vice-President, Research and Innovation and its partners, to understand objectives, project requirements and timelines. Communicates effectively with team members, clients and other stakeholders throughout the production process to ensure alignment with project goals and deliverables. Presents ideas and project updates to stakeholders in a clear and professional manner. Project management: Delivers simultaneous video projects on time and within budget. Effectively manages own time and resources to meet deadlines and production milestones. Demonstrates efficiency in workflow and production processes by optimizing productivity without compromising quality. Implements feedback from colleagues and stakeholders to continuously improve the impact and effectiveness of video content. Asset management: Maintains and operates video equipment, including cameras, lighting equipment and audio devices. Stays updated on industry trends and technological advancements to enhance video production capabilities. Oversees the organization and proper storage of video assets in the University\u2019s media library. What you will bring:\u00a0 Bachelor\u2019s degree in film, communications, or a related field; an equivalent combination of education and work experience will be considered. Minimum of three years of relevant experience in video production Ability to conceptualize and develop unique story ideas\u00a0 Ability to operate cameras effectively, adjust settings, and capture high-quality footage Skilled in framing shots, selecting angles, and using lighting to enhance storytelling and evoke emotion Strong proficiency in video editing software, such as Adobe Premiere, Final Cut Pro and other tools from the Adobe Creative Suite Knowledge of best practices for lighting setups and audio recording\u00a0 Proven understanding of the steps required to produce exceptional video content Strong communication and collaboration skills Strong organizational skills to manage multiple projects, deadlines and priorities simultaneously Ability to adapt to changing circumstances, unexpected challenges and evolving project requirements Ability to work efficiently under pressure while maintaining attention to detail and quality standards Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Senior-Advisor--Program-and-Learner-Support--PGME-_JR40380": {"title": "Senior Advisor, Program and Learner Support (PGME)", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: New Position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 24 Job Family: Academic Administration # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Postgraduate Medical Education Campus: Roger Guindon Hall Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 11 Salary Range: $95,594.00 - $120,750.00 About the Faculty of Medicine Welcome to the University of Ottawa Faculty of Medicine, the first and largest bilingual medical faculty in Canada. Our learners have access to state-of-the art facilities and specialized institutions as they learn alongside world-class physicians and scientists. Consistently ranked among the top 10 medical schools in Canada, our faculty is among the top faculties of medicine in the world for research impact in biomedical and health sciences. Position Purpose: Reporting to the Director, PGME, the Senior Advisor, Program and Learner Support, is responsible for the oversight and management of highly complex, high-risk cases for trainees in difficulty. Their responsibilities include the administration of emails, letters and documents relating to remediations, accommodations, probations, suspensions, dismissals, appeals, behavioural contracts and professionalism concerns. They coordinate advice between various internal and external offices, and governing bodies supporting PGME and hold primary accountability for ensuring that complex academic and administrative decisions are defensible, compliant, and aligned with institutional, legal, and accreditation requirements. During peak operational periods, they also complete program registration for the residents, ensuring compliance with requirements from both the university and our clinical partners. Specific Accountabilities: Management of trainees in difficulty: \u00a0 Oversees a portfolio of highly complex, high-risk cases involving trainees in significant difficulty, including monitoring remediation and probation plans, reassessments, extensions, reintegration\u2019s, behavioural agreements, performance milestones, and critical deadlines to ensure timely, defensible, and policy-compliant outcomes. As a key decision-maker determines when appropriate team members should convene to ratify decisions\u00a0based on case complexity, urgency, risk, and mandated timelines, applying and interpreting policies and procedures to guide the process. Ensures high-stakes decisions such as suspensions, dismissals, pay instructions, hospital privileges, removal from call schedules are clearly communicated and actioned as required. They enhance the quality of administrative tasks and processes in each of these unique cases, including writing and revising policies, procedures, guidelines and terms of reference documents when required. Liaises with internal and external legal counsel to determine appropriate and risk-mitigated approaches for managing unique and precedent-setting cases. Drafts clear, precise, and legally defensible communications to trainees, programs, or legal team, including formal notices related to performance, professionalism, remediation, and outcomes, ensuring alignment with institutional policy and legal requirements. \u00a0 Manages complex cases related to trainee wellness, collaborating with wellness, accommodation, and compensation experts to ensure accurate registration, documentation, and continuity of training. Works closely with accommodation specialists to ensure requirements are appropriately integrated into learning plans and operationalized, and communicated to hospitals, Program Directors and the Director of Academic Support. Coordinates with the Ontario Medical Association Physician Health Program (OMAPHP) to determine appropriate assessments and interventions as required. \u00a0 Serves as a central strategic liaison and trusted advisor with internal and external partners\u00a0including the College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario (CPSO), Professional Association of Residents of Ontario (PARO), Faculty Affairs, Professional Affairs, Program Directors, Payroll, Wellness Services, Accommodations Specialists, Registration Team, clearly communicating policies, consequences, and outcomes, influencing consistent decision-making and policy application across programs. \u00a0 Manages high stakes and sensitive professionalism cases, coordinating input and decisions from PGME leadership, legal counsel, and program directors. Synthesizes complex, sensitive information and competing considerations across legal, academic and operational domains to support timely, defensible outcomes and accountable for ensuring decisions are effectively communicated and operationalized. \u00a0 Management of Trainee Registration: Conducts registration tasks as required during peak periods, including: Enrollment & Compliance; Record Management; Advisory & Certification; Academic Evaluation; Stakeholder Coordination and \u00a0 Admission. \u00a0 Knowledge, Experience, Competencies, and Skills: Essential Qualifications : University Degree in Education, Administration, business or similar specialty or an equivalent combination of education and work experience. Minimum of 5 years of demonstrated experience in a similar role. Knowledge of the general structure of postgraduate medical education training in Canada and Ontario provincial licensing requirements. Other Qualifications: General knowledge of project management and administration principals normally acquired through postsecondary education and/or a work environment. Experience writing, interpreting, explaining and applying policies, regulations and procedures. Strong interpersonal and relationship building skills. Ability to communicate effectively with a variety of internal and external parties at all levels, including legal teams, professional affairs, licensing bodies; High level of accuracy and attention to detail; Knowledge of FIPPA and privacy legislation Advanced multi-tasking and organizational skills and ability to be flexible when priorities shift; Strong ability to work and problem solve under pressure; Able to work independently and have autonomy of their workload, establish priorities to meet deadlines in a fast-paced and high-volume work environment; Previous experience in a medical, health science and/or academic setting;\u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Knowledge of health care system in Ontario and Canada Advanced knowledge and experience using computer systems and software such as but not limited to: student and document management systems (uoCampus, Brightspace, Elentra, One45, XTender); word-processing (MS Word), financial spreadsheets (MS Excel), email and scheduling (MS Outlook), virtual conference platforms (MS Teams and Zoom), information databases and web-based applications; Bilingualism\u2014French and English (spoken and written) #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Senior-Officer--Academic-Administration_JR40350": {"title": "Senior Officer, Academic Administration", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Temporary replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 20 Job Family: Academic Administration # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Academic Administration Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 08 Salary Range: $68\u00a0149,00 - $86\u00a0083,00 About the Common Law Section of the Faculty of Law Over the past 20 years, the Common Law Section of the Faculty of Law at the University of Ottawa has become a national and global leader in many fields. We are the only law school to offer a common law degree in both English and French, and our faculty is one of the most accomplished in the country. We are an engaged Faculty, and we have a strong culture of public engagement. We also have a strong bond with the legal community in Ottawa and a special relationship with the Supreme Court of Canada. We are leaders -- leaders in research, leaders in the profession and leaders in our community. On any given day, members of our faculty may be seen testifying before a Parliamentary committee, providing expert commentary in the media, or contributing to public policy debates on the issues of the day. We are the most-cited law faculty in the national media. Through our dedication we have built strong experiential learning opportunities for our students where our students have unparalleled access to, and interaction with, the highest judges in the land to help foster an inclusive and high-quality student experience.\u00a0 Position Purpose: Serves as a first point of contact for students by receiving, acknowledging, anticipating and responding to various student requests and by ensuring above average client service in order to attract, retain and maintain the student population rate in all programs of study. Execute administrative tasks, such as evaluating requests, making recommendations, approving certain requests and providing follow-ups with students, colleagues and other faculties. As an ambassador for the University and the Faculty, attend graduation ceremonies and various events to promote the University of Ottawa and the Faculty\u2019s services. In this role, your responsibilities will include: Assess student needs: Evaluate student needs by analyzing, identifying, interpreting and translating student needs to determine which policies and procedures need to be activated. Admissions: Analyze student files with conditional acceptance and/or equivalences, so the Academic Development and Student Retention Specialist can follow up with these students. Monitor these files to ensure that administrative and academic deadlines are respected. Modification to program of study: Serve as a first point of contact for students by receiving, acknowledging and taking action on student requests for modification to their selected program of study. Course selection and registration application process: Serve as a first point of contact for students by receiving and acknowledging student requests for course selection and registration. Determine the eligibility of the request. Based on eligibility, approve course selection for first year students and special students and perform the necessary steps to finalize requests. What you will bring: Knowledge of administration usually acquired through postsecondary studies and several years of experience in a similar role. Knowledge of academic programs at the high school, college, undergraduate and postgraduate levels Knowledge of and experience interpreting, communicating and applying procedures, policies and methods Experience researching and analyzing data as well as creating administrative and statistical reports Experience in problem solving and decision-making Experience providing client service Experience implementing strategies and activity plans for students Experience with Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Access), word processing, Windows and Internet Capacity to deal with conflicting priorities and respect strict deadlines in a fast paced environment Organizational and analytical skills, excellent judgment and attention to detail Listening skills and strong work ethics Bilingual \u2013 French and English (spoken and written) Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Senior-Officer--Finance_JR40348": {"title": "Senior Officer, Finance", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 868N/A Job Family: Finance (JF) # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Finances, Common Law Section Campus: Main Campus Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/22 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/02 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 08 Salary Range: $68\u00a0149,00 - $86\u00a0083,00 About the Common Law Section of the Faculty of Law Over the past 20 years, the Common Law Section of the Faculty of Law at the University of Ottawa has become a national and global leader in many fields. We are the only law school to offer a common law degree in both English and French, and our faculty is one of the most accomplished in the country. We are an engaged Faculty, and we have a strong culture of public engagement. We also have a strong bond with the legal community in Ottawa and a special relationship with the Supreme Court of Canada. We are leaders -- leaders in research, leaders in the profession and leaders in our community. On any given day, members of our faculty may be seen testifying before a Parliamentary committee, providing expert commentary in the media, or contributing to public policy debates on the issues of the day. We are the most-cited law faculty in the national media. Through our dedication we have built strong experiential learning opportunities for our students where our students have unparalleled access to, and interaction with, the highest judges in the land to help foster an inclusive and high-quality student experience. Position purpose: Reporting to the Finance Manager , the incumbent plans and coordinates the activities related to the financial operations of the sector, including payroll verification and financial transactions in order to provide support and advice to managers and to ensure that the policies and procedures are well understood and properly implemented. In this role, your responsibilities will include:\u00a0 Administering internally and/or externally-funded budgets to ensure compliance with established policies. Analyzing financial statements against budgets for various types of funds to identify and address any inconsistencies. \u00a0 Performing all research and analysis necessary to provide recommendations to managers and/or researchers on the effective preparation of budgets, budget projections and shortfalls to maximize revenue and contain expenditure. Administering the ongoing financial transaction processes for the sector, to maintain financial integrity and transparency and to ensure compliance with established policies. Acting as approval agent for invoices, travel advances, requests for reimbursement and credit card statements to verify that documentation and taxation are correct. Preparing requests for remuneration, and required documentation for the generation of contracts, and administer remuneration for casual and term employees to ensure compliance with established policies and to minimize the possibility of a negative impact on the affected employee, such as an interruption in pay. Verifying payroll reports for accuracy and liaise with Payroll Services to reconcile any inconsistencies. Purchasing goods and services for the sector, on purchase order or on credit card, in compliance with all relevant policies. Requesting estimates from suppliers on behalf of internal clients and provide recommendations. Maintaining an effective filing and records management system to preserve confidentiality of the records and ensure that complete and accurate documentation is available for future reference or audit purposes. What you will bring: Advanced knowledge of accounting principles normally acquired through postsecondary studies in a related discipline or an equivalent combination of education and work experience. Minimum 3 years of demonstrated experience in a similar role Knowledge of computer systems and software including financial systems, spreadsheets, databases, Internet and e-mail Experience in interpreting, explaining and applying policies and regulations Experience in preparing reports Ability to produce high quality work under pressure while meeting strict deadlines Strong interpersonal and communication skills Strong organizational skills High level of discretion Bilingualism \u2013 French and English (spoken and written) Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Research-Assistant_JR40389": {"title": "Research Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2026 Fall Semester | Trimestre d'automne Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des sciences sociales / Faculty of Social Sciences Unit: Institute of Feminist and Gender Studies_Kathryn Trevenen_JM Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Recherche / Research Course Title: N/A Course Code: N/A Section: Supervisor name (if known): Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/21 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/03 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: N/A Work Start Date: November 15, 2026 Work End Date: December 22, 2026 Total Work Hours: 65 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: N/A Language of Work: Anglais | English, Fran\u00e7ais | French Requirements and Nature of Work: Nature of Work: Research Assistant for a project on feminist pedagogies exploring creative writing as part of graduate study. Qualifications: PhD student with expertise in feminist research methodology and pedagogy. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Research-Assistant_JR40376": {"title": "Research Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2027 Winter Semester | Trimestre d'hiver Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des sciences sociales / Faculty of Social Sciences Unit: School of Sociological and Anthropological Studies_Elke Winter_JM Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Recherche / Research Course Title: N/A Course Code: N/A Section: Supervisor name (if known): Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/21 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/03 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: N/A Work Start Date: January 15, 2027 Work End Date: April 30, 2027 Total Work Hours: 120 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: N/A Language of Work: Anglais | English Requirements and Nature of Work: Nature of Work: Lead student researcher for a project on social citizenship and skilled migration. Assistance is needed for literature syntheses, Endnote reference management software, research ethics, quantitative (statistical analysis, e.g. SPSS, Stata) and qualitative research design (semi-structured interviews, NVivo-assisted analysis), data collection, and analysis, as well as for project management and team calibration. Qualifications: Doctoral studies in sociology or related discipline, experience in quantitative and qualitative research methods, excellent writing skills in English are a MUST, knowledge of German and French are assets. Student must be able to commit to staying on the project for a minimum of 2 years. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/CUPE---Winter-2027---Research-Assistant---ASalyzyn---CML_JR40373-1": {"title": "CUPE - Winter 2027 - Research Assistant - A.Salyzyn - CML", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2027 Winter Semester | Trimestre d'hiver Faculty: Section de Common Law / Common Law Section Unit: Common Law _ST Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Recherche / Research Course Title: N/A Course Code: N/A Section: Supervisor name (if known): Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/21 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/05 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: N/A Work Start Date: janvier 04, 2027 Work End Date: avril 30, 2027 Total Work Hours: 140 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: N/A Language of Work: Anglais | English Requirements and Nature of Work: Nature of work Professor Salyzyn is seeking a research assistant to support an educational program for high school students, run in partnership with a not-for-profit organization. The program helps students learn about access to justice and the risks and benefits of technological solutions for addressing the public\u2019s legal needs. More information about the program is available https://ojen.ca/en/resource/apps-for-justice-program/. The anticipated work includes drafting a new legal scenario for use in the program\u2019s modules and supporting uptake in schools. Minimum Qualifications The student applying for this position must be currently enrolled in a JD or graduate program at the Faculty of Law. Applications *must* include: (1) the student's most recent transcript; (2) a cover letter indicating the student's experience with academic research and writing, as well as the student's interest in the use of technology in the legal system; and (3) the name and contact information of one academic reference. Applications without these three supporting documents will not be considered. Previous experience working with youth in educational settings is an asset. Language requirements: English This contract is will be starting in November 2026. To be considered for this position, please make sure to also apply to the posting for the fall 2026 session (JR40368). Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/CUPE---Fall-2026---Research-Assistant---ASalyzyn---CML_JR40368": {"title": "CUPE - Fall 2026 - Research Assistant - A.Salyzyn - CML", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2026 Fall Semester | Trimestre d'automne Faculty: Section de Common Law / Common Law Section Unit: Common Law _ST Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Recherche / Research Course Title: N/A Course Code: N/A Section: Supervisor name (if known): Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/21 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/05 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: N/A Work Start Date: novembre 11, 2026 Work End Date: d\u00e9cembre 31, 2026 Total Work Hours: 60 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: N/A Language of Work: Anglais | English Requirements and Nature of Work: Nature of work Professor Salyzyn is seeking a research assistant to support an educational program for high school students, run in partnership with a not-for-profit organization. The program helps students learn about access to justice and the risks and benefits of technological solutions for addressing the public\u2019s legal needs. More information about the program is available https://ojen.ca/en/resource/apps-for-justice-program/. The anticipated work includes drafting a new legal scenario for use in the program\u2019s modules and supporting uptake in schools. Minimum Qualifications The student applying for this position must be currently enrolled in a JD or graduate program at the Faculty of Law. Applications *must* include: (1) the student's most recent transcript; (2) a cover letter indicating the student's experience with academic research and writing, as well as the student's interest in the use of technology in the legal system; and (3) the name and contact information of one academic reference. Applications without these three supporting documents will not be considered. Previous experience working with youth in educational settings is an asset. Language requirements: English This contract is meant to be extended until April 30, 2027. To be considered for this position, please make sure to also apply to the posting for the winter 2027 session (JR40373). Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/APTPUO---Hiver-2027---PED4600-P09---STAGE-II-MILIEU-SCOL-LMENT_JR40365-1": {"title": "APTPUO - Hiver 2027 - PED4600 P09 - STAGE II MILIEU SCOL. \u00c9L\u00c9MENT.", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location: Main Campus Academic Period: 2027 Winter Semester Faculty: Facult\u00e9 d'\u00e9ducation / Faculty of Education Academic Unit: Formation \u00e0 l\u2019enseignement (Fac. Education) Course Title: Stage II en milieu scolaire \u00e9l\u00e9mentaire Course Code: PED4600 Section: P09 Course Description: Gestion, prise en charge et analyse de l'enseignement aux cycles primaire et moyen. Approfondissement des diff\u00e9rents aspects de la profession enseignante. Retour r\u00e9flexif sur les notions th\u00e9oriques et sur les exp\u00e9riences r\u00e9alis\u00e9es dans le cadre des stages et des s\u00e9minaires d'int\u00e9gration. \u00c9laboration d'un dossier professionnel d'apprentissage. Le stage a lieu du 04 janvier au 23 avril 2027. Mode d'enseignement: Cours hybride en pr\u00e9sentiel, alternant pr\u00e9sentiel et distance Posting limited to: Professeur \u00e0 temps-partiel r\u00e9gulier / Regular Part-Time Professor Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/21 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/11/22 Expected Enrolment: 20 Approval date: 2026/10/21 Number of credits: 3 Work Hours: 39 Hourly Rate: Enseignement / Teaching: $239.47 (2024-2025) The academic year starts on September 1 and ends on August 31. These rates do not included vacation pay nor statutory pay. These rates will be applied until a new collective agreement is ratified. Retro will be paid after the ratification. Course type: D Posting type: R\u00e9gulier / Regular Language of instruction: Fran\u00e7ais | French Competence in second language: Passive Course Schedule: Jeudi | Thursday 17:30-20:30 Jeudi | Thursday 17:30-20:30 Vendredi | Friday - Cours offer en mode hybride en pr\u00e9sentiel Requirements: Objectif du poste: Le programme de formation \u00e0 l\u2019enseignement de la Facult\u00e9 d\u2019\u00e9ducation de l\u2019Universit\u00e9 d\u2019Ottawa recrute des professeures conseill\u00e8res et de professeurs conseillers pour accompagner des stagiaires en enseignement aux cycles primaire, moyen, interm\u00e9diaire et sup\u00e9rieur. Ce r\u00f4le s\u2019inscrit dans une d\u00e9marche de formation pratique visant \u00e0 soutenir le d\u00e9veloppement professionnel des futurs enseignants. Responsabilit\u00e9s principales du poste: La professeure conseill\u00e8re ou le professeur conseiller aura pour mission, entre autres, de : \u2756 Offrir un accompagnement p\u00e9dagogique et un soutien affectif favorisant le d\u00e9veloppement progressif des comp\u00e9tences professionnelles en enseignement, \u00e0 travers des r\u00e9troactions constructives, cibl\u00e9es et adapt\u00e9es au niveau du stagiaire ; \u2756 Mettre en place des modalit\u00e9s efficaces de supervision \u00e0 distance, en utilisant des outils num\u00e9riques appropri\u00e9s et en veillant au respect des principes li\u00e9s \u00e0 l\u2019\u00e9thique et \u00e0 la confidentialit\u00e9; \u2756 Soutenir activement l\u2019enseignante accompagnatrice ou l\u2019enseignant accompagnateur afin d\u2019assurer une coh\u00e9rence et une compl\u00e9mentarit\u00e9 dans l\u2019encadrement du stagiaire ; \u2756 Planifier, r\u00e9aliser et documenter des s\u00e9ances d\u2019observation, ainsi que des rencontres de r\u00e9troaction en dyade et en triade \u00e0 distance, en s\u2019appuyant sur des outils num\u00e9riques facilitant l\u2019analyse r\u00e9flexive ; \u2756 Participer \u00e0 la s\u00e9ance d\u2019information obligatoire le 7 janvier 2027 (10 h \u00e0 12 h) . \u2756 Animer les s\u00e9minaires de stage aux dates suivantes : Jeudi 14 janvier 2027 - 17h30 \u00e0 20h20 (Mode AU) Jeudi 18 f\u00e9vrier 2027 - 17h30 \u00e0 20h20 (Mode AU) Vendredi 23 avril 2027 - 17h30 \u00e0 20h20 (En Pr\u00e9sentiel) \u2756 Attribuer la note sommative du stage et entrer la note sommative du stage dans le registre pr\u00e9vu \u00e0 cet effet au plus tard dans les 10 jours suivant la fin du stage. Qualifications minimales requises pour le poste: - Ma\u00eetrise en \u00e9ducation. - Qualification dans un domaine li\u00e9 \u00e0 l'enseignement dans les \u00e9coles de langue fran\u00e7aise en Ontario. - Exp\u00e9rience r\u00e9cente dans l'enseignement ou l'administration scolaire au niveau primaire/junior/\u00e9l\u00e9mentaire. - Connaissance du programme d'\u00e9tudes de l'Ontario, des normes de pratique de la profession enseignante et du code de d\u00e9ontologie de l'Ordre des enseignantes et des enseignants de l'Ontario. - L'universit\u00e9 peut prendre en consid\u00e9ration une exp\u00e9rience ou des qualifications \u00e9quivalentes \u00e0 celles mentionn\u00e9es ci-dessus. Un niveau d'\u00e9ducation/d'exp\u00e9rience jug\u00e9 \u00e9quivalentes pourrait suppl\u00e9er aux exigences d'\u00e9ducation et/ou d'exp\u00e9rience requises. Si on vous invite \u00e0 poursuivre les \u00e9tapes du processus de s\u00e9lection, veuillez nous aviser de tout besoin n\u00e9cessitant des mesures d'adaptation. Les renseignements communiqu\u00e9s seront trait\u00e9s avec respect et confidentialit\u00e9. Les employ\u00e9(e)s sont tenus(e) par la loi provinciale de mener \u00e0 bien des formations obligatoires. La liste des formations peut \u00eatre modifi\u00e9e par une loi provincial. Additional Information and/or Comments: An acceptable level of education and/or experience could be viewed as being equivalent to the educational required and/or demonstrated experience. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any adaptive measures you might require. Information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. Employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training. The list of training may be modified by provincial law. The hiring process will be governed by the current APTPUO collective agreements; you can click here for the main unit , here for the OLBI unit , or here for the Toronto/Windsor unit to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact vra.affairesprofessorales@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/APTPUO---Hiver-2027---PED4600-P10---STAGE-II-MILIEU-SCOL-LMENT_JR40366": {"title": "APTPUO - Hiver 2027 - PED4600 P10 - STAGE II MILIEU SCOL. \u00c9L\u00c9MENT.", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time