fficacit\u00e9; Grande attention aux d\u00e9tails et capacit\u00e9 \u00e0 corriger de fa\u00e7on rigoureuse les calculs et les d\u00e9marches de r\u00e9solution; Moyenne cumulative minimale (C.G.P.A.) de 8 Note minimale de A- dans ADM2341 ou l\u2019\u00e9quivalent (cours d\u2019un minimum de 3 cr\u00e9dits requis); Les candidats doivent d\u00e9montrer qu\u2019ils ont compl\u00e9t\u00e9 au moins trois cours en comptabilit\u00e9; Exp\u00e9rience en enseignement en ligne consid\u00e9r\u00e9e comme un atout; Exp\u00e9rience avec les plateformes de vid\u00e9oconf\u00e9rence (p. ex. Microsoft Teams) consid\u00e9r\u00e9e comme un atout; Bonne capacit\u00e9 d'expression et de communication en fran\u00e7ais; Les candidats devront d\u00e9montrer qu'ils ont au moins 3 cours en comptabilit\u00e9; Une excellente connaissance en Excel \u00a0\u00a0 Commentaires a pr\u00e9f\u00e9rence sera accord\u00e9e aux candidats ayant d\u00e9j\u00e0 occup\u00e9 un poste d\u2019assistant(e) d\u2019enseignement ou de correcteur(trice) pour ce cours; La pr\u00e9f\u00e9rence sera accord\u00e9e aux candidats inscrits \u00e0 un programme de Telfer; Les candidats pr\u00e9s\u00e9lectionn\u00e9s pourraient \u00eatre invit\u00e9s \u00e0 une entrevue avec le professeur ou le coordonnateur du cours; Les heures du contrat pourraient \u00eatre ajust\u00e9es en fonction de l\u2019horaire et des disponibilit\u00e9s; Veuillez noter que vous devez avoir acc\u00e8s \u00e0 un ordinateur, une connexion Internet, un microphone et une cam\u00e9ra; Les heures du contrat peuvent \u00eatre r\u00e9parties diff\u00e9remment entre les t\u00e2ches, selon les besoins du professeur au cours de la session; Les candidats doivent \u00eatre situ\u00e9s \u00e0 Ottawa pour toute la dur\u00e9e du cours (\u00e0 l\u2019exception des jours f\u00e9ri\u00e9s officiels de l\u2019Universit\u00e9), y compris pour la correction de l\u2019examen final et le calcul des notes finales; Veuillez noter que la m\u00e9thode d\u2019enseignement ou de travail peut \u00eatre modifi\u00e9e pour des raisons de sant\u00e9 ou autres; \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/CUPE--Winter-2027--TA---ADM744_JR40059": {"title": "CUPE -Winter 2027- TA - ADM744", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2027 Winter Semester | Trimestre d'hiver Faculty: \u00c9cole de gestion Telfer / Telfer School of Management Unit: Telfer School of Management_Students and Casuals Job Classification: Teaching Assistant/Demonstrator/Lab Monitor (CUPE) Posting Type: Cours pr\u00e9cis / Specific course Course Title: Fiscalit\u00e9 II Course Code: ADM4744 Section: Supervisor name (if known): TieMei Li Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/19 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/31 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:30 Correction - Notation / Grading:33 Surveillance / Proctoring:7 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: n/a Work Start Date: January 04, 2027 Work End Date: April 30, 2027 Total Work Hours: 70 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: In person Language of Work: Anglais | English Requirements and Nature of Work: Naature du travail Corriger les examens de mi-session et les questionnaires. Surveiller les examens. Tenir des heures de bureau. Effectuer des t\u00e2ches administratives. Prendre connaissance de tout le mat\u00e9riel sur Brightspace, comprendre le d\u00e9roulement du cours, consulter le manuel et ses exercices, et renforcer les recommandations de la professeure ou du professeur pour aider les \u00e9tudiants \u00e0 r\u00e9ussir. Comprendre la d\u00e9marche menant \u00e0 chaque r\u00e9ponse des examens de mi-session. Rencontrer individuellement les \u00e9tudiants pour revoir leur examen et leur offrir un soutien adapt\u00e9. Pr\u00e9server la confidentialit\u00e9 du contenu des examens. Expliquer \u00e0 chaque \u00e9tudiant les erreurs commises \u00e0 l\u2019examen de mi-session et la d\u00e9marche menant aux bonnes r\u00e9ponses. Formuler et communiquer des recommandations concernant l\u2019attribution de points partiels, conform\u00e9ment aux directives de la professeure ou du professeur. Aider la professeure ou le professeur, au besoin, dans les t\u00e2ches administratives et les autres t\u00e2ches li\u00e9es au cours. Les rencontres avec les \u00e9tudiants auront lieu sur le campus, sauf si des restrictions de sant\u00e9 publique l\u2019interdisent. Qualifications minimales Solide compr\u00e9hension de la fiscalit\u00e9. Capacit\u00e9 \u00e0 appliquer des grilles de correction et \u00e0 fournir une r\u00e9troaction claire et structur\u00e9e. Capacit\u00e9 \u00e0 respecter des \u00e9ch\u00e9ances strictes et \u00e0 g\u00e9rer efficacement sa charge de travail. Moyenne pond\u00e9r\u00e9e cumulative minimale de 7,0. Avoir r\u00e9cemment termin\u00e9 le cours ADM 4344, ou son \u00e9quivalent, avec une note minimale de A\u2212. Une exp\u00e9rience de l\u2019enseignement en ligne est un atout. Une exp\u00e9rience des plateformes de vid\u00e9oconf\u00e9rence, comme Microsoft Teams, est un atout. Commentaires La pr\u00e9f\u00e9rence sera accord\u00e9e aux personnes ayant obtenu les meilleures notes, \u00e0 celles ayant d\u00e9j\u00e0 \u00e9t\u00e9 assistantes d\u2019enseignement ou correctrices pour ce cours, ainsi qu\u2019aux personnes inscrites \u00e0 un programme de Telfer. \u00c0 priorit\u00e9 d\u2019embauche \u00e9gale, la pr\u00e9f\u00e9rence sera accord\u00e9e aux personnes ayant d\u00e9j\u00e0 corrig\u00e9 pour le cours ADM 4344 ou son \u00e9quivalent. Les personnes pr\u00e9s\u00e9lectionn\u00e9es pourraient \u00eatre convoqu\u00e9es \u00e0 une entrevue avec la professeure ou le professeur responsable du cours, ou avec la coordonnatrice ou le coordonnateur. Le nombre d\u2019heures affich\u00e9 pourrait augmenter de 10 % \u00e0 30 % selon les inscriptions. Vous devrez avoir acc\u00e8s \u00e0 un ordinateur, \u00e0 Internet, \u00e0 un microphone et \u00e0 une webcam. Les heures pr\u00e9vues au contrat peuvent \u00eatre r\u00e9parties diff\u00e9remment entre les t\u00e2ches au cours du trimestre, selon les besoins de la professeure ou du professeur. Vous devez vous trouver dans la r\u00e9gion d\u2019Ottawa pendant toute la dur\u00e9e du cours, sauf durant les cong\u00e9s officiels de l\u2019Universit\u00e9. Cette exigence s\u2019applique jusqu\u2019\u00e0 la correction de l\u2019examen final et au calcul des notes finales par la professeure ou le professeur. Les modalit\u00e9s d\u2019enseignement ou de travail pourraient changer pour des raisons de sant\u00e9 ou pour d\u2019autres raisons. Please note, you will be required to have access to a computer, internet, microphone and webcam. Contract hours may be reallocated among different tasks, which the professor may adjust over the course of the semester. Must be located in Ottawa for the entire duration of the course (with the exception of official University holidays), including through the grading of the final exam and calculation of final grades by the professor. Please note that the method of teaching or working may change due to health or other reasons. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Intermediate-Coordinator--Administrative-Support_JR40251": {"title": "Intermediate Coordinator, Administrative Support", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): N/A Job Family: Administrative Support # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Pathology Administration Campus: Roger Guindon Hall Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/19 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/30 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 07 Salary Range: $62,027.00 - $78,349.00 About the Faculty of Medicine Welcome to the University of Ottawa Faculty of Medicine, the first and largest bilingual medical faculty in Canada. Our learners have access to state-of-the art facilities and specialized institutions as they learn alongside world-class physicians and scientists. Consistently ranked among the top 10 medical schools in Canada, our faculty is among the top faculties of medicine in the world for research impact in biomedical and health sciences. Position Purpose: Responsible for executing logistical activities related to the delivery of programs within a faculty, service or research environment, including but not limited to courses, workshops, training and events, while ensuring smooth and efficient operations and an exceptional client experience. Coordinates elements of physical and material resources, including arrangements with third-party providers and other uOttawa services. Performs routine financial transactions, routine communications and other administrative support functions related to program delivery. May be required to execute logistical activities for special projects within the scope of the overall program. Typical Accountabilities: Program Operations: Performs a variety of operational functions related to ongoing program delivery in the context of a service, academic sector, or in a research environment. Performs routine administrative and financial functions. Executes all operational activities around program delivery efficiently and within budget. Troubleshoots unanticipated complications within the scope of program execution and implements creative solutions to ensure operational continuity. Maintains accurate records for key historical data and program metrics. Client Service and Communications: Acts as communication liaison between program management, clients, contributors, experts and other stakeholders in order to ensure the effective sharing of critical information related to the program components under his or her area of responsibility. Ensures that accurate and clear information related to the program, deadlines, and requirements is consistently available and updated. Addresses client feedback, escalates more complex areas of concern to program management, with the goal of favouring service excellence. Activity and Event Registration: Handles registration related to ongoing participation in program offerings. Plans and executes efficient participant registration and all related followup for special events. Handles other routine activities in support of the smooth execution of program events within the scope of the program. Resource Coordination: Administers accurate inventories of resources required for program delivery. Contributes to the overall planning for all program offerings by projecting resource requirements for program cycles, including material and space requirements. Reporting: Provides a variety of reports to management in support of program planning and continuous improvement initiatives. Knowledge, Experience and Skills: Essential Qualifications: Post-secondary education in administration, or an acceptable combination of education and experience Minimum of 3 years\u2019 relevant experience in administrative coordination Other Qualifications: Demonstrated experience in client service Experience working in a postsecondary institution or other complex organization is an asset Skills in the use all Microsoft Office software Skills in the use of web content management applications is an asset Strong organizational abilities and consistent attention to detail in a fast-paced environment Ability to produce high quality work in an environment with multiple strict deadlines Strong interpersonal and communication skills Demonstrated success working in a team setting Ability to occasionally work outside of normal office hours Ability to occasionally travel outside of the region Bilingualism \u2013 French and English (spoken and written) #LI-ONSITE Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Intermediate-Specialist--Academic-Administration_JR40288": {"title": "Intermediate Specialist, Academic Administration", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: Temporary replacement of a regular position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): 12 Job Family: Academic Administration # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: SEPH Operations Campus: Roger Guindon Hall Union Affiliation: SSUO Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/19 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/30 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: SSUO Grade 10 Salary Range: $85,040.00 - $107,421.00 About the Faculty of Medicine Welcome to the University of Ottawa Faculty of Medicine, the first and largest bilingual medical faculty in Canada. Our learners have access to state-of-the art facilities and specialized institutions as they learn alongside world-class physicians and scientists. Consistently ranked among the top 10 medical schools in Canada, our faculty is among the top faculties of medicine in the world for research impact in biomedical and health sciences. Position Purpose: Supports the educational dossier of the School of Epidemiology and Public Health (SEPH), with special attention to the Master of Public Health (MPH), master\u2019s and PhD programs (EPI) and Undergraduate Medical Education (UGME) program activities. The incumbent provides administrative support to the program leads to organize and track educational activities and to support the development and implementation of new initiatives. Serves as back up for the Lead Officer, Academic Administration, Residency Program in Public Health, and Preventative Medicine (PHPM). Specific Accountabilities: Plans and implements the practicum placement program, and additional practicum opportunities for all SEPH students, by anticipating and coordinating the logistical needs of upcoming and ongoing placements; this includes expectations, timelines, documentation, ensuring each student and placement supervisor completes the practicum learning contract and ensuring the collection of completed (student) evaluation forms and practicum evaluation forms. Presents to the students the practicum information and process of how to apply to a practicum in separate information sessions. Communicates and advises participating students, professors and community partners on practicum opportunities, responsibilities, and best practices within their practicum experience. Reviews each placement posting and supervisor CV to ensure they meet the criteria for a suitable learning experience. Assists the student or community partner with arising issues related to their placement. Alerts the Program Director of issues as necessary including tracking the timely organization of practicum placements by individual students. Develops and actively maintains working relationships with community partners in public health and related organizations. Actively seeks out new opportunities for funded practicum opportunities for students. Regularly meets/communicates with key community partners and is assigned to collect and analyze their feedback and level of satisfaction with the practicum program and to identify opportunities to deepen the working relationship. Makes recommendations as required to improve and maintain SEPH systems and/or processes leading to mutually beneficial working relationships with the broader community. Coordinates program activities and events including (but not limited to): orientation, student research day, MPH Capstone, and program committee meetings, Ph.D. proposal presentations. Reports operational and technical difficulties or problems to the Manager on a \u00a0regular basis. Coordinates faculty advising for the MPH program and collection/analysis of advising reports. Liaise with career services to develop and host workshops and other support as needed for students. Provides feedback, and proactively collaborates with the Program Director and Manager on necessary program improvements. Assists the Program Director in developing program guidelines and policies, updates procedures and recommends proper tools to ensure that goals are met or surpassed. Proactively and in response to requests, updates guidance documents and verifies web links for all program components. Includes planning and developing the information support resources needed to respond to students and faculty regarding program changes. Maintains and updates the EPI and MPH Brightspace pages. Coordinates the MPH Committee and Epidemiology Graduate Studies Committee meetings; MPH and Epidemiology Admissions Committees, PhD comprehensive exam Committee meetings, is the point of contact for committee members, ensuring all documentation, and information is provided. Attending all graduate program committee meetings taking and writing minutes, preparing related materials, following up on action items as required. Knowledge, Experience and Skills: Essential Qualifications: An undergraduate degree or an acceptable equivalent combination of education or experience; Minimum 4 years in the areas of community development, or delivery of medical/health educational programs or in an academic institution; Other Qualifications: Experience in developing and maintaining successful community partnerships and stakeholder relations. A demonstrated commitment to client service, strong interpersonal skills Excellent organizational and time management skills and strong ability to multi-task and prioritize responsibilities Excellent negotiating and problem-solving skills, attention to details Strong analytical skills to compile and analyze information and data for program planning Ability to work independently with minimal supervision, ability to work successfully in teams Demonstrated technical software skills using MSOffice Suite, proficiency using email and internet; experience with database considered an asset Ability to work occasional evenings and weekends Bilingual (French and English). #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Manager--Operations---Minwagoming-Centre-for-Indigenous-Health-Research-and-Education_JR40211": {"title": "Manager, Operations - Minwagoming Centre for Indigenous Health Research and Education", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nFollow us on LinkedIn Posting Reason: New Position Job Type: Employee Anticipated Duration in Months (for contracts and temporary assignments): N/A Job Family: Business Operations # of Open Positions: 1 Faculty/Service - Department: Strategic Planning and Implementation Campus: Roger Guindon Hall Union Affiliation: N/A Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/19 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/30 Hours per week: 35 Salary Grade: Non-Union Grade NM1 Salary Range: $107,503.00 - $134,379.00 About the Faculty of Medicine Welcome to the University of Ottawa Faculty of Medicine, the first and largest bilingual medical faculty in Canada. Our learners have access to state-of-the art facilities and specialized institutions as they learn alongside world-class physicians and scientists. Consistently ranked among the top 10 medical schools in Canada, our faculty is among the top faculties of medicine in the world for research impact in biomedical and health sciences. Position Purpose: Provides strategic and operational leadership for the Minwagoming Centre for Indigenous Health Research and Education and is accountable for its effective administration, resource management, program delivery, and day-to-day operations. Serves as the central operational lead for the coordination and integration of Indigenous academic and learner-related activities across the Faculty of Medicine\u2019s educational portfolios, supporting Indigenous learners throughout their educational journey, including Undergraduate Medical Education (UGME), Postgraduate Medical Education (PGME), graduate education, and other relevant health education programs. Translates the Centre\u2019s strategic priorities into coordinated operational plans and initiatives that advance Indigenous health research, education, reconciliation, and meaningful engagement with First Nations, Inuit, and M\u00e9tis communities. Supports the Faculty of Medicine\u2019s strategic priorities and commitments to reconciliation, Indigenous health, and social accountability through the Centre\u2019s programs, services, partnerships, and learner supports. Ensures the effective integration of people, resources, programs, projects, partnerships, and administrative functions to support a coordinated and cohesive Indigenous learner experience across the Faculty. Fosters collaborative relationships across the Faculty of Medicine, the University, affiliated health-care organizations, Indigenous communities, governments, and external partners. Specific Accountabilities: Operational Leadership: Provides leadership for the day-to-day operations of the Centre by developing operational plans, establishing priorities, coordinating activities, monitoring performance, and ensuring the efficient and effective delivery of programs, services, and initiatives aligned with the Centre\u2019s mandate. Coordinates briefing materials, reports, committee documentation, and operational updates for senior leadership, advisory committees, and external partners. Academic and Educational Leadership: Provides strategic and operational leadership for the Centre\u2019s academic and educational mandate and oversees Indigenous education and learner-related activities across the Faculty\u2019s educational portfolios, including UGME, PGME, graduate education, and other relevant programs. Provides operational oversight of the Indigenous Undergraduate Medical Education (UGME) Program and works collaboratively with the Indigenous leaders within the Faculty, program directors, academic and administrative leaders, faculty members, learners, Elders, Knowledge Holders, Indigenous communities, and relevant Faculty and University partners to ensure the effective coordination, integration, and continuity of Indigenous academic programming and learner supports. Leads and supports the planning, implementation, evaluation, and continuous improvement of Indigenous health education, curriculum initiatives, learner experiences, faculty development, mentorship, recruitment and retention, and other academic activities, ensuring alignment with the Centre\u2019s mandate, Faculty priorities, accreditation requirements, Indigenous ways of knowing, cultural safety, and Indigenous community engagement. Identifies gaps, emerging needs, and opportunities; develops integrated processes, resources, and operational practices; facilitates communication and coordination across programs and portfolios; and monitors learner needs, program outcomes, and operational effectiveness to strengthen Indigenous learner recruitment, admissions, transition, progression, wellness, community connection, and successful completion across the educational continuum. Strategic Planning: Leads the implementation of the Centre's strategic priorities through annual operational planning, project management, performance measurement, and continuous quality improvement. Monitors progress toward objectives and recommends operational improvements to enhance effectiveness. Partnership and Community Engagement:\u00a0 Develops and maintains collaborative relationships with Indigenous communities, Elders, Knowledge Holders, researchers, learners, faculty members, affiliated hospitals, governments, Indigenous organizations, and community partners to support the Centre's priorities and ensure initiatives are community-informed and culturally responsive. Program and Project Management:\u00a0 Directs the operational planning and delivery of multiple concurrent projects related to Indigenous health education, research, community engagement, and reconciliation initiatives. Establishes timelines, allocates resources, manages risks, monitors deliverables, and evaluates outcomes. Human Resources Management: Manages the Centre's administrative and professional staff, including workforce planning, recruitment, onboarding, performance management, coaching, professional development, workload allocation, succession planning, and employee engagement. Fosters an inclusive, respectful, and culturally safe workplace consistent with university policies and Indigenous values. Financial Management:\u00a0 Develops and manages the Centre's operating and project budgets. Monitors expenditures, prepares financial forecasts, authorizes expenditures within delegated authority, ensures compliance with University financial policies, funding agreements, and reporting requirements, and recommends resource allocation to support operational priorities. Continuous Improvement: \u00a0 Identifies opportunities to improve operational efficiency, client service, organizational effectiveness, and administrative processes. Implements innovative practices that strengthen the Centre's capacity to deliver high-quality programs and services. Communications and Events:\u00a0 Oversees operational planning for meetings, ceremonies, educational activities, cultural events, community gatherings, and knowledge-sharing initiatives. Coordinates communications that promote the Centre's activities and strengthen engagement across the Faculty and Indigenous communities. Knowledge, Experience and Skills: Essential Qualifications: Undergraduate degree in Indigenous Studies Public Administration, Business Administration, Health Sciences, Education, or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum of seven (7) years' experience working at a post-secondary institution, government agency and/or community organization in the area of Indigenous education, community services and programs. Minimum of two (2) years of management experience in operations, administration, financial management, project management, human resources. Public event and protocol management. Lived Indigenous experience and extensive knowledge and understanding of Indigenous culture, history, education, protocols and practices learned through active Indigenous community engagement. Experience working with Indigenous communities, organizations, governments, academic institutions, health systems, or research environments. \u00a0 Other Qualifications: Extensive knowledge and understanding of Indigenous history, culture, traditions, education, as well as Indigenous community resources and services Experience in developing organizational performance measurement frameworks (setting objectives, targets, strategies, initiatives, performance indicators) Demonstrated success and strong business and partnership development skills. Proven ability to create long-term and mutually beneficial relationships are essential Excellent communication skills to interact with various partners. Ability to clearly present information in writing to internal and external parties Demonstrated experience leading multidisciplinary teams and managing organizational operations. Experience managing complex budgets and multiple concurrent projects. Motivated and driven to identify and develop new opportunities Excellent organizational skills Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work in a team and cross-cultural environment Bilingualism in English and French (oral and written) Knowledge of an Indigenous language an asset #LI-Hybrid Key Competencies at uOttawa: Here are the required competencies for all or our employees at uOttawa:\u00a0 Planning: Organize in time a series of actions or events in order to realize an objective or a project. Plan and organize own work and priorities in regular daily activities. Initiative: Demonstrate creativity and initiative to suggest improvements and encourage positive results. Is proactive and self-starting. Show availability and willingness to go above and beyond whenever it is possible. Client Service Orientation: Help or serve others to meet their needs. This implies anticipating and identifying the needs of internal and external clients and finding solutions on how to meet them. Teamwork and Cooperation: Cooperate and work well with other members of the team to reach common goal(s). Accept and give constructive feedback. Able to adjust own behaviour to reach the goals of the team. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact hrtalentmanagement@uottawa.ca to communicate the accommodation need. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Note: if this is a union position: The hiring process will be governed by the current collective agreement related to the union affiliation noted above; you can click here to find out more. If this is a front-line position with responsibilities to interact with students, selected candidates must be rated at the Low Advanced proficiency level or higher for both oral comprehension and reading comprehension in their second official language. The rating is determined by a proficiency test designed by the Official Languages and Bilingualism Institute. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}, "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Research-Assistant_JR40292": {"title": "Research Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nPosting Reason: New Position Location where work is to be performed: Main Campus Session: 2026 Fall Semester | Trimestre d'automne Faculty: Facult\u00e9 des sciences sociales / Faculty of Social Sciences Unit: \u00c9cole sup\u00e9rieure d'affaires publiques et internationales Job Classification: Research Assistant (CUPE) Posting Type: Recherche / Research Course Title: N/A Course Code: N/A Section: Supervisor name (if known): Date Posted (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/16 Applications must be received BEFORE (YYYY/MM/DD): 2026/10/29 Description of tasks (hours): Unless specified below, specific allocation of hours to various tasks should be articulated in a description of work negotiated and signed by you and the supervising professor prior to the commencement of work. Recherche / Research:0 Pr\u00e9paration / Preparation:0 Contact avec \u00e9tudiants / Contact with Students:0 Correction - Notation / Grading:0 Surveillance / Proctoring:0 Formation / Training:0 Autre / Other:0 Number of positions: 1 Expected Enrolment: N/A Work Start Date: October 30, 2026 Work End Date: December 22, 2026 Total Work Hours: 115.7 Hourly Rate: AE-RA-Tuteur / TA-RA-Tutor : $33.83 ou/or $53.31 (Sept. 2026 - Aug. 2027) Undergraduate students will be paid at the lower rate; while graduate students will be paid at the higher rate. These rates do not include vacation pay nor statutory pay. Method of Delivery: N/A Language of Work: Anglais | English Requirements and Nature of Work: Nature of Work: The student will support a SSHRC-funded research project examining the design of green procurement policies across Canada and their effects on firm-level innovation outcomes. The student will perform the following tasks: \u2022 Data collection, entry, and cleaning. \u2022 Producing descriptive graphs and figures. \u2022 Assisting with regression analysis. \u2022 Organizing and verifying program code and raw data files. Assisting with the preparation of replication packages. \u2022 Assisting with formatting and copy-editing research manuscripts. Qualifications: Prior graduate-level training in natural resources management, environmental policy, environmental governance, or environmental economics. \u2022 Strong quantitative research skills, including fluency in R and/or Stata programming language. Working knowledge of Excel and of SQL is an asset. \u2022 Interest in learning and applying computational approaches for policy text analysis. \u2022 Interest in learning about the field of policy process/policy design. \u2022 Interest in learning about the field of green innovation policy. Additional Information and/or Comments: All University of Ottawa employees are required under provincial law to successfully complete all mandatory legislated training offered by the University. The list of training requirements may be modified by provincial law. If you are invited to continue the selection process, please notify us of any particular adaptive measures you might require. We may consult with the Health and Wellness sector of Human Resources, if needed. Any information you send us will be handled respectfully and in complete confidence. The hiring process will be governed by the current CUPE 2626 collective agreement; you can click here to find out more. The University of Ottawa embraces diversity and inclusion in the workplace. We are passionate about our people and committed to employment equity. We foster a culture of respect, teamwork and inclusion, where collaboration, innovation, and creativity fuel our quest for research and teaching excellence. While all qualified persons are invited to apply, we welcome applications from qualified Indigenous persons, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women and LGBTQIA2S+ persons. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible, barrier-free work environment. The University is also committed to working with applicants with disabilities requesting accommodation during the recruitment, assessment and selection processes. Applicants with disabilities may contact the academic unit to communicate the accommodation need. Prior to May 1, 2022, the University required all students, faculty, staff, and visitors (including contractors) to be fully vaccinated against Covid-19 as defined in Policy 129 \u2013 Covid-19 Vaccination. This policy was suspended effective May 1, 2022 but may be reinstated at any point in the future depending on public health guidelines and the recommendations of experts."}}, "rows": {"https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor---Emerging-Leader-in-Arctic-governance--Indigenous-leadership--community-resilience--or-related-fields_JR38816-1": {"school_id": 1625, "posting_url": "https://uottawa.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uOttawa_External_Career_Site/job/Ottawa-ON/Assistant-Professor---Emerging-Leader-in-Arctic-governance--Indigenous-leadership--community-resilience--or-related-fields_JR38816-1", "job_title_in_p